1
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:55,050
Number five, go a bit tighter.

2
00:00:55,540 --> 00:00:56,650
Out on two.

3
00:00:57,500 --> 00:00:59,450
Through the kitchen on six.

4
00:00:59,940 --> 00:01:02,290
Single on four. That's it.

5
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:04,450
One, let them come through you.

6
00:01:04,540 --> 00:01:06,790
And then bring them in through,
camera three ready.

7
00:01:07,795 --> 00:01:08,279
Cut!

8
00:01:08,380 --> 00:01:10,460
Right, can we get this place
clean, please? Come on.

9
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:13,690
Let's have a look at the cleaning.

10
00:01:13,780 --> 00:01:16,850
Carole, that's nowhere near clean enough,
come on, get a move on!

11
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:19,500
I don't wanna hear it, just do it.

12
00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:21,850
- It's nowhere near clean enough.
- Call that clean?

13
00:01:21,940 --> 00:01:23,770
Uh, hello? She's talking to you!

14
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:25,740
Are you listening to me?
It's not clean enough!

15
00:01:27,380 --> 00:01:28,970
I've had enough!

16
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:31,250
No, Ben, come out. Come out!

17
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:32,700
Just get on with it, will you, eh?

18
00:01:32,705 --> 00:01:35,610
- Are you listening to me?
- Are you listening to me? Just do it!

19
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:37,981
- I said enough, you understand?
- On four now. That's it.

20
00:01:38,071 --> 00:01:39,671
You've got no choice. You're not master.

21
00:01:39,676 --> 00:01:41,690
Don't you talk to me!

22
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:42,975
Oh, who am I gonna talk to, then?

23
00:01:42,980 --> 00:01:44,340
You've got to do what you're told.

24
00:01:44,430 --> 00:01:46,820
Just get on with it, will you?
There's your bucket, go on.

25
00:01:46,910 --> 00:01:48,410
I've had enough of this!

26
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:51,890
Roy! Roy!

27
00:01:51,980 --> 00:01:53,620
Yeah, everything's under control, Amanda.

28
00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:55,210
Bring up six.

29
00:01:55,300 --> 00:01:56,820
We'll handle it and then let you know.

30
00:01:56,910 --> 00:01:58,410
- Yeah, just give it a second, okay?
- Go to three.

31
00:01:58,615 --> 00:01:59,670
Bring up three.

32
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:01,210
John, loosen on that.

33
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:03,090
- Oh, don't, he's gonna cry!
- You're stupid!

34
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:05,050
No, don't! He's gonna cry.

35
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:06,620
Don't cry.

36
00:02:08,340 --> 00:02:10,570
- Go to three!
- Shut up, I know what I'm doing.

37
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:13,050
- I'm going to kill you all.
- Oh, this is stupid.

38
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:15,780
I hate you. You are all degenerates.

39
00:02:15,860 --> 00:02:18,050
I hate you all!

40
00:02:19,420 --> 00:02:22,090
Ben, go to single on that. Now cut to six.

41
00:02:23,620 --> 00:02:24,940
Quit winding him up.

42
00:02:27,580 --> 00:02:29,130
That's it. Good. Just stay on that.

43
00:02:29,220 --> 00:02:30,330
Take it easy. Take it easy.

44
00:02:30,420 --> 00:02:32,250
Get off me!

45
00:02:39,620 --> 00:02:41,450
Don't wind him up.

46
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:44,450
Oh, look, he's moaning!

47
00:02:46,260 --> 00:02:47,660
I think we should stop this, Keith.

48
00:02:47,750 --> 00:02:50,190
You wanna tell the channel we
killed their best ratings ever?

49
00:02:50,195 --> 00:02:54,170
No, it's fantastic TV, but I'm not sure
Tendai can take much more.

50
00:02:54,260 --> 00:02:56,250
You wanna pull him out?

51
00:02:57,140 --> 00:02:59,650
I expect he'll calm down. Right, cut to ten.

52
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:02,730
Who you got on ten, Stevie Wonder?
Cut to nine!

53
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:10,250
Stop them, Roy, shut it down!

54
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:11,660
It's great telly, Amanda.

55
00:03:11,740 --> 00:03:13,530
Roy, he's gonna blow!

56
00:03:14,220 --> 00:03:15,930
Well, don't ask him!

57
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:18,890
Stop it! Stop it!

58
00:03:20,780 --> 00:03:23,650
- Oi, listen to me!
- That's enough.

59
00:03:23,740 --> 00:03:26,010
Roy, I'm putting the guards in.

60
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:29,690
Security to the kitchen!

61
00:03:30,260 --> 00:03:32,770
That's enough! That's enough!

62
00:03:43,740 --> 00:03:45,890
Or what? Or what?

63
00:03:46,580 --> 00:03:47,580
Or what?

64
00:03:47,700 --> 00:03:49,570
Don't talk to me like that.

65
00:03:49,660 --> 00:03:51,170
Oh, my God!

66
00:03:55,340 --> 00:03:57,530
Shut down transmission!
Shut down transmission!

67
00:03:57,620 --> 00:03:59,770
Shut it down, now!

68
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:13,050
Oh, Bette Kidman, the MP,
returned your call, Judge.

69
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:14,620
She's happy to meet with you.

70
00:04:14,700 --> 00:04:16,100
Does she know why I want to meet?

71
00:04:16,180 --> 00:04:18,290
- Well, she seemed to.
- Hmm.

72
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:19,450
Maybe she's ahead of me.

73
00:04:19,540 --> 00:04:21,410
She'd prefer to meet at the House of Commons.

74
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:23,170
Okay. Come on.

75
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:24,770
That ogre, Kidman,

76
00:04:24,860 --> 00:04:27,770
is examining the overspend on the navy's
new radar equipment.

77
00:04:27,860 --> 00:04:30,660
That's what the Public Accounts Select
Committee does, Home Secretary.

78
00:04:30,665 --> 00:04:34,090
I have no problem with that.
But why is she meeting with Deed?

79
00:04:34,180 --> 00:04:37,170
Oh, the Home Office has its spies, too, Ian.

80
00:04:37,340 --> 00:04:40,330
Deed will rake over the dead embers of that
ridiculous accusation made

81
00:04:40,420 --> 00:04:41,900
by a totally unreliable witness.

82
00:04:41,980 --> 00:04:44,460
I mean, it's an obsession!
How many times do I have to say it?

83
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:46,410
There was no bribe.

84
00:04:48,780 --> 00:04:51,810
Not even Mrs Kidman would dare to raise that.

85
00:04:52,460 --> 00:04:56,370
Little Bette Kidman is no friend of mine,
nor the government's.

86
00:04:56,460 --> 00:05:00,500
Egged on by this busy body judge,
who knows where she'll go?

87
00:05:04,060 --> 00:05:09,650
John, Ian Rochester asked me to offer you the
TV Dungeon trial.

88
00:05:09,740 --> 00:05:12,501
He's just spent the past month trying
to get me thrown off the bench.

89
00:05:12,506 --> 00:05:16,180
Now he offers me the trial most judges
would give their ears for. Why?

90
00:05:16,260 --> 00:05:18,650
He's looking for someone who can take the heat.

91
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:21,130
The press already seems to
have delivered its verdict.

92
00:05:21,220 --> 00:05:24,730
The DCA is under pressure
to have the trial filmed.

93
00:05:24,820 --> 00:05:25,890
Not in my court.

94
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:28,250
Oh, is that a yes, John?

95
00:05:28,340 --> 00:05:31,450
Does that mean the issue of my removal is ended?

96
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:36,170
You're going to meet the
Chair of the Public Accounts Committee.

97
00:05:36,260 --> 00:05:38,410
You're well informed, Monty.

98
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:40,210
Oh, be careful, John.

99
00:05:40,300 --> 00:05:43,490
Mrs Kidman might seem friendly,
but she's treacherous.

100
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:45,530
I've got nowhere else to go.

101
00:05:45,620 --> 00:05:49,730
The allegation against Haughton and Listfield
must be investigated.

102
00:05:50,060 --> 00:05:52,330
Do you have any evidence, John?

103
00:05:55,100 --> 00:05:58,250
We can't have trial by Select Committee.

104
00:05:59,100 --> 00:06:01,530
Perhaps you should concede defeat.

105
00:06:12,060 --> 00:06:15,810
Will you look into Neil Haughton's relationship
with Tim Listfield?

106
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:18,850
The remit of the Committee
is to look at the overspend,

107
00:06:18,940 --> 00:06:20,570
why the contract went over budget.

108
00:06:20,660 --> 00:06:23,300
Yeah, there have been
allegations that the overspend

109
00:06:23,380 --> 00:06:26,410
was due entirely to double and triple billing.

110
00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:30,050
Do you have evidence of any fraudulent behaviour?

111
00:06:30,140 --> 00:06:36,090
Rufus Barron was Tim Listfield's PA
during the period of the radar contract.

112
00:06:36,180 --> 00:06:38,210
He collected a lot of evidence.

113
00:06:38,300 --> 00:06:40,730
- What happened to it?
- Tim Listfield made it disappear,

114
00:06:40,820 --> 00:06:43,090
a large number of packing boxes.

115
00:06:43,180 --> 00:06:45,010
Is that speculation?

116
00:06:45,500 --> 00:06:46,820
Entirely.

117
00:06:48,140 --> 00:06:50,410
What sort of documentation was in the boxes?

118
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:52,210
Copies of invoices.

119
00:06:54,620 --> 00:06:56,810
What happened to Mr Barron?

120
00:06:58,140 --> 00:07:00,600
It appears he committed suicide.

121
00:07:01,820 --> 00:07:03,490
There was a note.

122
00:07:04,260 --> 00:07:06,330
- You don't believe it.
- No.

123
00:07:10,020 --> 00:07:13,130
Did you see the transcript of the statement
that I took from Mr Barron?

124
00:07:13,220 --> 00:07:16,410
- No. Why would we?
- Because I gave it to the Attorney.

125
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:18,130
Didn't he show it to you?

126
00:07:18,220 --> 00:07:19,930
He's a member of the government.

127
00:07:21,860 --> 00:07:25,570
Sir John, the sole object is to discover
why a new radar system

128
00:07:25,660 --> 00:07:27,450
for the navy was over budget.

129
00:07:27,540 --> 00:07:31,130
I gave Rufus Barron's diaries
to the Attorney General.

130
00:07:32,140 --> 00:07:35,920
They contained details of the
meetings where Listfield

131
00:07:36,020 --> 00:07:39,370
allegedly bribed Haughton.

132
00:07:43,780 --> 00:07:45,260
What do you want from this, Sir John?

133
00:07:45,340 --> 00:07:49,380
I want a criminal prosecution of Tim Listfield
and Neil Haughton,

134
00:07:49,820 --> 00:07:51,450
if he's involved.

135
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:56,170
Do you have anything other than the diaries?

136
00:07:58,300 --> 00:07:59,330
No.

137
00:08:01,740 --> 00:08:05,780
Then it looks like the allegation may have died
with Mr Barron.

138
00:08:30,260 --> 00:08:31,410
This is a surprise.

139
00:08:31,500 --> 00:08:33,570
Yeah. Looking for Charlie.

140
00:08:34,220 --> 00:08:35,620
Wrong room.

141
00:08:39,380 --> 00:08:41,820
I'd offer you a cup of tea, but I've got a con
with the CPS.

142
00:08:41,900 --> 00:08:43,930
Yeah, your clerk told me.

143
00:08:45,180 --> 00:08:47,370
Did you know I was hearing the Dungeon case?

144
00:08:47,460 --> 00:08:49,500
I thought High Court judges
were squabbling for it.

145
00:08:49,580 --> 00:08:50,850
Exactly.

146
00:08:51,540 --> 00:08:53,170
Something's up.

147
00:08:53,540 --> 00:08:54,890
Like what?

148
00:08:57,180 --> 00:08:59,770
- Did you see the killing on TV?
- No.

149
00:09:00,460 --> 00:09:01,460
Doubtless I will.

150
00:09:01,540 --> 00:09:03,340
Yeah, well, you must be one of the few, John.

151
00:09:03,345 --> 00:09:04,825
That's probably why you got the case.

152
00:09:05,740 --> 00:09:07,690
It's going to be tough to empanel a jury.

153
00:09:07,780 --> 00:09:09,000
Oh, yes.

154
00:09:11,060 --> 00:09:13,170
Shall I pick you up for dinner?

155
00:09:13,260 --> 00:09:15,210
I've got a dinner date.

156
00:09:17,580 --> 00:09:19,450
Better find Charlie.

157
00:09:24,180 --> 00:09:27,170
Did Rufus Barron ever talk about his friends?

158
00:09:27,260 --> 00:09:29,290
He didn't kill himself, Dad.

159
00:09:29,380 --> 00:09:30,730
Evidence?

160
00:09:31,740 --> 00:09:34,170
He was scared, but he wasn't suicidal.

161
00:09:34,780 --> 00:09:36,490
He wanted to help.

162
00:09:37,620 --> 00:09:39,890
Do you know what you're implying?

163
00:09:40,740 --> 00:09:43,050
I don't really want to go there,

164
00:09:43,140 --> 00:09:44,930
but I liked Rufus.

165
00:09:45,980 --> 00:09:47,890
Can't you do something?

166
00:09:59,780 --> 00:10:01,930
I've never seen a dead body.

167
00:10:03,020 --> 00:10:04,730
Well, if you'd rather not.

168
00:10:05,180 --> 00:10:06,500
Sir John.

169
00:10:08,420 --> 00:10:10,450
- I'm Dr Graceman.
- Yes, I remember you.

170
00:10:10,540 --> 00:10:13,690
- You've given evidence in my court.
- On several occasions.

171
00:10:13,780 --> 00:10:15,820
You want another view about
the death of Mr Barron.

172
00:10:15,825 --> 00:10:19,210
Yes, I had Mrs Cooper call you because I thought
you'd be impartial.

173
00:10:19,300 --> 00:10:21,410
I'd hope all pathologists are.

174
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:24,690
Well, counsel here, this is Charlie Deed,

175
00:10:24,780 --> 00:10:26,450
thinks that the original pathologist

176
00:10:26,540 --> 00:10:29,810
might cling to his position, regardless.

177
00:10:32,540 --> 00:10:34,570
Would you care to see the body?

178
00:10:41,420 --> 00:10:43,330
Sorry I'm late. Left my mobile in court.

179
00:10:43,420 --> 00:10:44,490
We've barely started.

180
00:10:44,580 --> 00:10:47,220
I'm surprised your phone wasn't stolen.

181
00:10:47,300 --> 00:10:50,850
So am I. Thieves must be
becoming less opportunistic.

182
00:10:51,420 --> 00:10:55,460
We've decided not to call Amanda Rogers,
the Dungeon floor manager.

183
00:10:55,660 --> 00:10:56,880
Isn't she key?

184
00:10:56,980 --> 00:10:58,650
She's unreliable.

185
00:10:59,140 --> 00:11:00,170
Hidden agenda?

186
00:11:00,260 --> 00:11:04,730
A relationship with the producer that ended badly,
and a suspected drug habit.

187
00:11:04,820 --> 00:11:06,250
I don't want any surprises.

188
00:11:06,340 --> 00:11:07,690
We've got Tendai Shoko.

189
00:11:07,780 --> 00:11:10,130
Has he been sentenced for his
manslaughter conviction yet?

190
00:11:10,220 --> 00:11:11,890
Awaiting reports.

191
00:11:11,980 --> 00:11:13,330
Who's the weaker defendant?

192
00:11:13,420 --> 00:11:15,650
Roy Storidge, the director.

193
00:11:15,740 --> 00:11:19,090
Put the pressure on him, he might go cutthroat.

194
00:11:19,180 --> 00:11:21,050
Right, let's look at the evidence.

195
00:11:42,340 --> 00:11:43,560
Charlie?

196
00:11:44,980 --> 00:11:47,170
I don't think this is Rufus.

197
00:11:50,780 --> 00:11:52,290
It's like him.

198
00:11:55,660 --> 00:11:59,770
Morticians occasionally get their charges
and paperwork mixed up.

199
00:12:06,620 --> 00:12:08,100
It's not him.

200
00:12:08,740 --> 00:12:10,690
Let me check elsewhere.

201
00:12:13,700 --> 00:12:15,100
Excuse me.

202
00:12:29,380 --> 00:12:31,170
He's in his 80s.

203
00:12:52,420 --> 00:12:53,770
This one?

204
00:12:56,380 --> 00:12:58,370
Thank goodness for that.

205
00:13:00,020 --> 00:13:01,770
Did he simply drown?

206
00:13:02,300 --> 00:13:06,370
The note says it was "a
massive inhalation of water".

207
00:13:06,460 --> 00:13:08,250
His lungs were full.

208
00:13:08,700 --> 00:13:10,810
Is that usual in drowning?

209
00:13:10,900 --> 00:13:15,730
There are no reliable post-mortem signs
which confirm death by drowning.

210
00:13:16,300 --> 00:13:19,490
That's what we assume in the
absence of anything else.

211
00:13:19,580 --> 00:13:22,570
He did have a lot of alcohol in his blood.

212
00:13:22,660 --> 00:13:24,570
Rufus didn't drink.

213
00:13:24,660 --> 00:13:28,770
Alcohol is often used as an
anaesthetic to make suicide painless.

214
00:13:29,420 --> 00:13:31,570
Has the coroner just got it wrong here?

215
00:13:31,660 --> 00:13:33,250
It's not likely.

216
00:13:33,940 --> 00:13:35,610
We should get a second autopsy.

217
00:13:35,700 --> 00:13:38,690
For that, you'd have to petition the coroner or
Home Office.

218
00:13:38,780 --> 00:13:40,730
Or a High Court judge.

219
00:13:42,220 --> 00:13:44,730
I can call for a second opinion.

220
00:13:44,820 --> 00:13:46,690
Oh, you can, Sir John?

221
00:13:47,620 --> 00:13:50,650
- I would have to inform the Home Office.
- Yes.

222
00:13:51,260 --> 00:13:54,930
And I would like you to perform
the autopsy, Dr Graceman.

223
00:13:57,620 --> 00:13:59,650
Do you have the authority?

224
00:14:01,140 --> 00:14:03,970
Either way, it will set some alarm bells ringing.

225
00:14:06,580 --> 00:14:09,490
I thought Deed was going to be occupied with
the TV Dungeon case.

226
00:14:09,580 --> 00:14:10,900
Thank you.

227
00:14:11,740 --> 00:14:12,860
We rather hoped he would be.

228
00:14:12,900 --> 00:14:15,540
Then why has he demanded a
second autopsy on Rufus Barron?

229
00:14:15,620 --> 00:14:17,650
He can't do that. That's for the Home Office.

230
00:14:17,740 --> 00:14:19,290
Well, it seems he's done it.

231
00:14:19,380 --> 00:14:21,130
The Home Office can undo it.

232
00:14:22,140 --> 00:14:24,600
And if I do, he'll call cover-up.

233
00:14:24,700 --> 00:14:26,260
If you can't control these judges, Ian,

234
00:14:26,265 --> 00:14:28,730
I'll put a bill before Parliament that will.

235
00:14:28,820 --> 00:14:31,490
Neil is terribly fond of you, Charlie.

236
00:14:31,940 --> 00:14:35,170
What you and your father are
doing is extremely hurtful.

237
00:14:35,260 --> 00:14:37,170
I'm not doing anything.

238
00:14:37,900 --> 00:14:40,970
Perhaps you should try telling Neil that, darling.

239
00:14:50,180 --> 00:14:51,180
- Excuse me.
- Sorry.

240
00:14:53,735 --> 00:14:55,615
Look, my only comment is, I'm entirely innocent

241
00:14:55,620 --> 00:14:57,930
and I expect the case to be thrown out of court.

242
00:14:58,020 --> 00:15:01,290
But, Mr Storidge, what about the family?
How do you feel about them?

243
00:15:02,420 --> 00:15:04,250
Mr Hodgekinson,

244
00:15:04,420 --> 00:15:07,490
how do you feel about the families?

245
00:15:11,820 --> 00:15:14,490
Can you comment, Mr Hodgekinson?

246
00:15:14,740 --> 00:15:16,330
I should not even be here.

247
00:15:16,420 --> 00:15:20,380
My doctors have told me that I am
not fit enough to stand trial.

248
00:15:21,260 --> 00:15:24,370
Mr Hodgekinson is suffering
complete nervous exhaustion.

249
00:15:24,460 --> 00:15:26,370
Due to too much coke?

250
00:15:29,140 --> 00:15:32,410
Because of the nature of
this case, it has generated

251
00:15:32,500 --> 00:15:35,690
a huge amount of public interest and debate.

252
00:15:35,780 --> 00:15:38,690
If media publication figures are to be believed,

253
00:15:39,660 --> 00:15:42,380
then the murder of Reggie Ross in The Dungeon

254
00:15:42,460 --> 00:15:46,130
was witnessed by 16.2 million people.

255
00:15:47,460 --> 00:15:51,890
Now, this doesn't necessarily mean
that these two defendants are guilty.

256
00:15:52,220 --> 00:15:56,810
And if any report in any
medium suggests otherwise,

257
00:15:57,380 --> 00:16:00,490
then I will hold the reporter, the paper and
the broadcaster in contempt.

258
00:16:00,580 --> 00:16:02,890
You make no mistake about that.

259
00:16:04,140 --> 00:16:07,850
You are welcome here, but
only if you obey the rules.

260
00:16:09,100 --> 00:16:11,890
Mr Mason Allen, I believe you have a submission.

261
00:16:12,180 --> 00:16:14,050
Yes, my lord.

262
00:16:15,140 --> 00:16:19,730
My client is not fit to stand trial
due to his unstable mental health.

263
00:16:19,820 --> 00:16:21,730
Yes, I read the doctor's reports.

264
00:16:21,820 --> 00:16:24,090
Are Mr Hodgekinson's psychiatrists in court?

265
00:16:24,180 --> 00:16:26,050
- They are, my lord.
- Very well.

266
00:16:26,140 --> 00:16:28,490
Then, I think the sensible way to proceed would be

267
00:16:28,580 --> 00:16:33,330
to swear a jury and hear his fitness to plead
before the substantive case.

268
00:16:33,420 --> 00:16:36,570
- Using the same jury, my lord?
- Yes, it's appropriate.

269
00:16:36,660 --> 00:16:41,130
No, if the first action fails,
the jury will be prejudiced.

270
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:46,970
He seems to have a very clear understanding of
what's going on.

271
00:16:47,060 --> 00:16:50,610
If I might have a brief word
with my instructing solicitor?

272
00:16:50,700 --> 00:16:51,810
I think you should.

273
00:17:02,060 --> 00:17:03,540
A non-starter, Jonathan.

274
00:17:03,620 --> 00:17:06,930
Insist on a separate jury, Newman.

275
00:17:07,020 --> 00:17:09,130
Not with this judge. He's ruled.

276
00:17:09,220 --> 00:17:11,370
I think Keith is very flaky.

277
00:17:12,900 --> 00:17:17,210
If that becomes apparent, we'll try
and stop the trial. All right?

278
00:17:26,140 --> 00:17:27,570
Um...

279
00:17:28,620 --> 00:17:32,090
I am instructed not to proceed
with the submission, my lord.

280
00:17:32,180 --> 00:17:33,890
Very well.

281
00:17:33,980 --> 00:17:36,370
Mr Norwalk, am I going to hear from you?

282
00:17:36,460 --> 00:17:39,100
We would argue that the case
against the second defendant,

283
00:17:39,180 --> 00:17:41,490
Roy Storidge, is based on fallacy.

284
00:17:41,580 --> 00:17:44,410
Although, he was directing the show at the time of
Mr Ross's death,

285
00:17:44,500 --> 00:17:49,210
it was not foreseeable that one inmate would use
lethal violence upon another.

286
00:17:50,020 --> 00:17:53,900
Before Mr Storidge knew what was happening,
it was too late.

287
00:17:55,740 --> 00:17:58,530
You argued for dismissal
before the directions judge

288
00:17:58,620 --> 00:18:02,660
and the submission failed.
- Why do you think it might succeed now?

289
00:18:02,740 --> 00:18:05,460
With your superior knowledge of the law, my lord,

290
00:18:05,540 --> 00:18:07,570
I'm sure you can see the argument's validity.

291
00:18:07,660 --> 00:18:08,980
Please.

292
00:18:09,060 --> 00:18:14,370
The prosecution successfully
argued that Mr Hodgekinson

293
00:18:14,460 --> 00:18:18,340
and Mr Storidge, as responsible
people for the production company,

294
00:18:18,420 --> 00:18:22,130
owed the inmates of The
Dungeon a clear duty of care.

295
00:18:30,340 --> 00:18:33,090
I am instructed to pursue this point, my lord.

296
00:18:36,140 --> 00:18:39,410
Your client seems to have
regained his mental acuity

297
00:18:39,500 --> 00:18:41,770
remarkably well, Mr Mason Allen.

298
00:18:41,860 --> 00:18:44,450
Does he know the law better than you do?

299
00:18:44,540 --> 00:18:45,570
It's possible.

300
00:18:45,660 --> 00:18:47,090
Yes, well, anything's possible.

301
00:18:47,180 --> 00:18:49,450
But the ruling by the directions judge, in this,

302
00:18:49,540 --> 00:18:52,610
seems to me both accurate and binding.

303
00:18:52,940 --> 00:18:56,210
We would argue that the situation
which pertained in The Dungeon,

304
00:18:56,300 --> 00:19:00,570
was one in which my client owed
a different standard of care

305
00:19:00,660 --> 00:19:03,220
from, say, fellow road users.

306
00:19:03,300 --> 00:19:07,650
The inmates had voluntarily exposed themselves
to a significant risk,

307
00:19:08,180 --> 00:19:10,410
like bungee jumpers.

308
00:19:10,500 --> 00:19:12,890
Yes, I think you know very well, Mr Mason Allen,

309
00:19:12,980 --> 00:19:16,810
that, with exceptions listed
in Brown, 1993, 2 All ER,

310
00:19:16,900 --> 00:19:20,170
page 75, one cannot escape conviction

311
00:19:20,260 --> 00:19:24,300
from a crime on the basis of the
victim's consent to the danger.

312
00:19:24,380 --> 00:19:27,690
I, too, enjoyed the law of torts at university.

313
00:19:30,620 --> 00:19:33,490
Now, are we ready to swear a jury?

314
00:19:33,580 --> 00:19:35,290
My lord,

315
00:19:36,420 --> 00:19:38,170
my client has

316
00:19:38,820 --> 00:19:42,210
serious reservations over jury selections.

317
00:19:42,300 --> 00:19:46,080
He is concerned to know how a
panel can be made up of anyone

318
00:19:46,180 --> 00:19:50,330
who hasn't been influenced by the programme
or subsequent coverage.

319
00:19:50,420 --> 00:19:52,010
Yes, it is a concern.

320
00:19:52,100 --> 00:19:56,090
We are just going to have to rely on
the jury's honesty and common sense,

321
00:19:56,180 --> 00:19:57,530
aren't we?

322
00:19:58,620 --> 00:20:00,050
Mr Clerk?

323
00:20:02,300 --> 00:20:05,490
Members of the jury panel, before
you're sworn in as jurors,

324
00:20:05,580 --> 00:20:08,530
I need to ask you a few questions. Now, firstly,

325
00:20:08,620 --> 00:20:12,730
did any of you see the death of
Mr Reggie Ross on television?

326
00:20:14,500 --> 00:20:15,690
Right.

327
00:20:17,775 --> 00:20:19,735
Thank you.
- Would you leave the jury box, please?

328
00:20:19,740 --> 00:20:21,540
Mr Clerk, would you bring in the next eight?

329
00:20:21,545 --> 00:20:23,025
Yes, my lord.

330
00:20:34,020 --> 00:20:37,370
My instructing solicitor
knows the man in the blazer.

331
00:20:38,540 --> 00:20:41,890
Thank you.
- Would you step out, please? Thank you.

332
00:20:49,980 --> 00:20:52,850
- I know you.
- Yes, Judge.

333
00:20:52,940 --> 00:20:55,010
I've appeared before you twice.

334
00:20:55,100 --> 00:20:56,810
- You're a barrister?
- Yes.

335
00:20:56,900 --> 00:21:00,010
Like everyone else, we now
get called for jury service.

336
00:21:00,100 --> 00:21:02,010
Yes, indeed.

337
00:21:02,100 --> 00:21:05,770
Do you know Mrs Mills or either
of the defence barristers?

338
00:21:06,660 --> 00:21:08,290
Not personally.

339
00:21:08,380 --> 00:21:11,650
Good. Mr Clerk, will you swear the jury, please?

340
00:21:11,740 --> 00:21:13,450
Certainly, my lord.

341
00:21:16,460 --> 00:21:19,930
Mr Shoko killed Reggie Ross in a fit of rage.

342
00:21:20,020 --> 00:21:23,330
He was prosecuted for murder, but
successfully raised the defence

343
00:21:23,420 --> 00:21:27,200
of provocation, and was convicted of manslaughter.

344
00:21:28,020 --> 00:21:31,010
More than half the country saw the killing.

345
00:21:31,100 --> 00:21:34,810
However, there is another kind
of guilt that we're trying here,

346
00:21:35,780 --> 00:21:37,850
that of the directing minds.

347
00:21:37,940 --> 00:21:41,490
And we will make the chain of
responsibility clear to you.

348
00:21:43,580 --> 00:21:46,770
Although Mr Ross's death was
brought about by Tendai Shoko,

349
00:21:46,860 --> 00:21:50,570
it was causally linked to
gross negligence on the part

350
00:21:50,660 --> 00:21:56,570
of Keith Hodgekinson and Roy Storidge,
who owed him a clear duty of care.

351
00:22:00,580 --> 00:22:03,650
Mr Hamling, can you tell the court
what the command structure was

352
00:22:03,740 --> 00:22:05,370
for The Dungeon?

353
00:22:05,780 --> 00:22:08,930
There's the controller of the
channel, who signs off on everything.

354
00:22:09,020 --> 00:22:11,530
Next, there's the head of
department at the channel.

355
00:22:11,620 --> 00:22:15,290
He commissions the production company.
I own the production company.

356
00:22:15,380 --> 00:22:19,010
Mr Hodgekinson is a producer.
He hired Mr Storidge.

357
00:22:20,460 --> 00:22:21,890
Why was the show commissioned?

358
00:22:21,980 --> 00:22:24,660
With the success of the first six series,
the channel automatically

359
00:22:24,700 --> 00:22:26,770
commissioned the seventh.

360
00:22:27,300 --> 00:22:29,250
How were the inmates selected?

361
00:22:29,340 --> 00:22:32,290
We've got a long interviewing
and auditioning process.

362
00:22:32,380 --> 00:22:33,780
Who did the interviews?

363
00:22:33,860 --> 00:22:36,090
Myself and Keith, along with psychologists,

364
00:22:36,180 --> 00:22:38,370
medical doctors, fitness experts.

365
00:22:38,460 --> 00:22:39,730
We formed the selection panel.

366
00:22:39,820 --> 00:22:42,690
Was the second defendant a
part of that process as well?

367
00:22:42,780 --> 00:22:46,450
Sure. He has to know how far
he can push the material.

368
00:22:46,540 --> 00:22:49,690
- The material?
- The inmates.

369
00:22:50,260 --> 00:22:52,820
And what was looked for in these inmates?

370
00:22:52,900 --> 00:22:57,330
People who could survive exciting,
lively, interesting, sexy challenges.

371
00:22:59,940 --> 00:23:01,290
Mrs Mills?

372
00:23:02,620 --> 00:23:05,930
Were there any health-and-safety procedures
put in place in this show?

373
00:23:06,020 --> 00:23:07,090
Yes.

374
00:23:07,660 --> 00:23:11,250
Mr Hodgekinson, the producer,
and the director, the second defendant,

375
00:23:11,340 --> 00:23:13,370
took those meetings and signed off on them.

376
00:23:13,460 --> 00:23:15,330
Were they ignored?

377
00:23:15,940 --> 00:23:17,650
I think they must have been.

378
00:23:17,740 --> 00:23:21,410
I certainly saw the assessment
of risks, and they were low.

379
00:23:23,020 --> 00:23:26,050
Did the channel commission the
seventh series on the same terms

380
00:23:26,140 --> 00:23:28,570
as all the others?
- No. They wanted some changes.

381
00:23:28,660 --> 00:23:29,690
Such as?

382
00:23:29,780 --> 00:23:33,010
They wanted it to be sexier,
livelier, more entertaining.

383
00:23:33,100 --> 00:23:36,010
More dangerous? More freaky? More scary?

384
00:23:36,660 --> 00:23:37,850
More challenging.

385
00:23:37,940 --> 00:23:41,250
Did you keep the power of veto in this process?

386
00:23:42,180 --> 00:23:43,890
Well, to some extent, yes.

387
00:23:43,980 --> 00:23:47,650
So, the defendants weren't the final arbiters

388
00:23:47,740 --> 00:23:50,050
on who took part in The Dungeon.

389
00:23:50,140 --> 00:23:52,290
- They certainly had a say.
- Ah!

390
00:23:52,380 --> 00:23:53,860
That's not answering my question.

391
00:23:53,940 --> 00:23:56,580
The controller at the channel
always has the final say.

392
00:23:56,660 --> 00:23:58,060
On your recommendation.

393
00:23:58,140 --> 00:24:01,290
As head of the production company,
that's usually the way it works, yes.

394
00:24:01,380 --> 00:24:03,610
Who has a say below you?

395
00:24:04,900 --> 00:24:06,010
Everybody has a voice.

396
00:24:06,100 --> 00:24:09,250
Keith, as the producer's, was the loudest,
next it's Roy.

397
00:24:09,340 --> 00:24:11,730
But he's the one who has to
force them into tight corners.

398
00:24:11,820 --> 00:24:13,090
To get them to explode?

399
00:24:13,180 --> 00:24:15,290
To help them to be more entertaining.

400
00:24:15,380 --> 00:24:16,490
To that end,

401
00:24:16,580 --> 00:24:20,250
did you order the two defendants to cause a fight
in The Dungeon?

402
00:24:20,340 --> 00:24:21,660
I did not.

403
00:24:22,140 --> 00:24:26,930
When Mr Cassidy and Mr Shoko had their first fight
in The Dungeon,

404
00:24:28,620 --> 00:24:30,930
what happened to the ratings?

405
00:24:31,020 --> 00:24:32,370
I'm not sure.

406
00:24:34,060 --> 00:24:36,250
Television is obsessed with ratings.

407
00:24:36,340 --> 00:24:39,530
Are you seriously telling this
jury you didn't check the numbers

408
00:24:39,620 --> 00:24:41,890
to find out what happened to them?

409
00:24:41,980 --> 00:24:44,290
- They went up.
- Oh, they shot up

410
00:24:44,860 --> 00:24:46,770
by 2.3 million!

411
00:24:47,460 --> 00:24:48,890
Yes, I believe they did.

412
00:24:48,980 --> 00:24:53,250
On that basis, was death the new benchmark
for entertainment?

413
00:24:53,860 --> 00:24:55,050
That's an obscene suggestion.

414
00:24:55,140 --> 00:24:58,410
Isn't it true you wanted social misfits?

415
00:24:58,500 --> 00:25:01,220
People who were, in the jargon, flaky,

416
00:25:01,540 --> 00:25:03,210
even unstable?

417
00:25:04,740 --> 00:25:06,930
- That would have been so irresponsible.
- Indeed!

418
00:25:07,020 --> 00:25:10,050
It might even have led to someone being killed.

419
00:25:14,980 --> 00:25:18,130
How much money did the
channel make from this show?

420
00:25:18,220 --> 00:25:19,620
I don't know. That's a secret.

421
00:25:19,700 --> 00:25:21,770
How much did your company make?

422
00:25:21,860 --> 00:25:25,330
- I'm not sure.
- Oh, come on, Mr Hamling, don't be coy.

423
00:25:25,820 --> 00:25:27,610
Was it 5,000?

424
00:25:28,340 --> 00:25:31,770
- Five million? Fifty million?
- No. Nothing like.

425
00:25:31,860 --> 00:25:34,770
It was £47 million, wasn't it?

426
00:25:39,500 --> 00:25:41,330
Answer the question.

427
00:25:42,580 --> 00:25:43,580
Yes.

428
00:25:43,660 --> 00:25:48,090
Does that include selling footage of the killing
to newspapers and TV,

429
00:25:48,180 --> 00:25:50,530
and the whole episode to Japanese television?

430
00:25:50,620 --> 00:25:53,650
- Did you sell that footage?
- It was a big news item.

431
00:25:53,740 --> 00:25:55,850
- Did you?
- Yes.

432
00:25:56,340 --> 00:25:59,650
That you helped manufacture
to rescue your failing show.

433
00:25:59,740 --> 00:26:02,050
A 37% audience share is not a failure.

434
00:26:02,140 --> 00:26:05,570
Up until the point in mid-October
when you ordered the shake-up in The Dungeon,

435
00:26:05,660 --> 00:26:07,890
you were only getting a 15% share,

436
00:26:07,980 --> 00:26:11,050
your rescue strategy being seriously dangerous.

437
00:26:11,140 --> 00:26:13,130
No. Just sexy and lively.

438
00:26:13,820 --> 00:26:16,970
Is that an e-mail you sent to Keith Hodgekinson?

439
00:26:29,260 --> 00:26:30,290
Yes.

440
00:26:30,380 --> 00:26:32,490
Sent during the third week of transmission.

441
00:26:32,820 --> 00:26:35,210
"Keith, push them into tight corners."

442
00:26:35,300 --> 00:26:38,330
"Make them way out, aggressive, freaky."

443
00:26:38,420 --> 00:26:40,340
Well, that's just colourful television language.

444
00:26:40,345 --> 00:26:43,330
"We've got to get the numbers up.
Pull out all the stops!"

445
00:26:43,420 --> 00:26:44,740
Well, that's what it's all about.

446
00:26:44,780 --> 00:26:48,170
When Misters Hodgekinson and Storidge
argued they were too freaky,

447
00:26:48,260 --> 00:26:51,290
that Mr Shoko was unstable, you overruled them.

448
00:26:52,420 --> 00:26:54,410
They knew what the rules were.

449
00:26:54,500 --> 00:26:57,490
They took all the health-and-safety checks.

450
00:26:57,580 --> 00:26:59,490
They just went beyond their remit.

451
00:26:59,580 --> 00:27:04,570
How interesting that it is not you or the panel
who is in the dock.

452
00:27:05,820 --> 00:27:09,170
Save your comments for closing
speeches, Mr Norwalk.

453
00:27:10,700 --> 00:27:13,730
The real change came with them putting
Liza in charge.

454
00:27:13,820 --> 00:27:17,490
No one wanted to take orders
from her, least of all Tendai.

455
00:27:17,980 --> 00:27:20,570
Some of those that were made
slaves refused to perform.

456
00:27:20,660 --> 00:27:22,250
What do you mean, not perform?

457
00:27:22,340 --> 00:27:24,340
Didn't the cameras capture you, whatever you did?

458
00:27:24,380 --> 00:27:26,810
Yeah, but if you're not acting
like the monkey in the cage,

459
00:27:26,900 --> 00:27:29,210
it doesn't make for great telly.

460
00:27:32,020 --> 00:27:33,020
Mrs Mills?

461
00:27:33,100 --> 00:27:35,560
- How long did this rebellion last?
- A day and a half.

462
00:27:35,660 --> 00:27:37,010
Were you punished?

463
00:27:37,100 --> 00:27:40,690
Everyone was. Slaves and slave masters.

464
00:27:40,780 --> 00:27:42,810
Did it have the desired effect?

465
00:27:42,900 --> 00:27:44,770
They locked the fridge.

466
00:27:44,860 --> 00:27:47,530
They shut off the electricity,

467
00:27:48,260 --> 00:27:50,330
put some of us in solitary.

468
00:27:50,820 --> 00:27:54,450
Everyone was really wound up
after about 18 hours of that.

469
00:27:54,860 --> 00:27:57,650
Liza was bricking it 'cause if she
didn't bring people into line,

470
00:27:57,740 --> 00:28:00,610
she'd be the next to be booted off the show.

471
00:28:01,140 --> 00:28:04,730
She got very bolshie, that's when Tendai freaked.

472
00:28:06,340 --> 00:28:08,330
How were the inmates kicked off the show?

473
00:28:08,500 --> 00:28:10,570
They devised games.

474
00:28:10,660 --> 00:28:14,970
We had to nick things from each other.
The one with the least stuff was out.

475
00:28:15,380 --> 00:28:20,330
But they could also chuck you out
if you didn't follow their orders.

476
00:28:20,940 --> 00:28:24,570
When Liza was put in charge, was
she acting on her own initiative?

477
00:28:24,660 --> 00:28:27,170
She was given orders by Cyclops,

478
00:28:27,460 --> 00:28:29,450
them behind the mirror.

479
00:28:29,820 --> 00:28:34,890
He told me that Tendai had been making homophobic
comments about me.

480
00:28:36,180 --> 00:28:38,690
It was like they wanted me
to pick a fight with him.

481
00:28:38,780 --> 00:28:41,890
- Was it always the same voice?
- No, they were different.

482
00:28:41,980 --> 00:28:45,170
- Like some disembodied Nazi.
- You're the Nazi!

483
00:28:45,260 --> 00:28:48,770
Oh, yeah? Well now who's facing
the war-crimes tribunal?

484
00:28:52,460 --> 00:28:57,970
Isn't it true, you decided to wind up
Tendai Shoko because of what he was?

485
00:28:58,820 --> 00:29:01,970
Well, the public wouldn't get
entertained if we sat around.

486
00:29:02,060 --> 00:29:04,970
So, you did decide to provoke him?

487
00:29:05,060 --> 00:29:07,250
He thought I was evil.

488
00:29:08,060 --> 00:29:10,650
He thought all queers are.
He's a born-again Christian.

489
00:29:10,740 --> 00:29:13,200
You're a died-in-the-wool racist.

490
00:29:15,140 --> 00:29:17,600
You are a racist, Mr Cassidy?

491
00:29:17,700 --> 00:29:19,130
No. I'm not.

492
00:29:22,060 --> 00:29:25,690
Of all the people to pick a fight with,
you chose Tendai.

493
00:29:26,620 --> 00:29:29,770
- Why was that?
- 'Cause he didn't like gays.

494
00:29:29,860 --> 00:29:30,860
Ah.

495
00:29:31,980 --> 00:29:34,540
Did you inform the panel

496
00:29:35,420 --> 00:29:36,970
that you're a member of the BNP?

497
00:29:37,060 --> 00:29:38,330
I'm not!

498
00:29:46,140 --> 00:29:49,810
- I must be a little confused, I mean,

499
00:29:50,860 --> 00:29:56,170
that is you at a BNP meeting
applauding the speaker, is it not?

500
00:30:00,700 --> 00:30:03,090
- That was a long time ago.
- Mmm-hmm.

501
00:30:03,180 --> 00:30:07,010
I was 16. My dad made me!

502
00:30:07,860 --> 00:30:09,780
- Why didn't we know about this?
- Good question.

503
00:30:09,785 --> 00:30:10,570
We had no idea.

504
00:30:10,660 --> 00:30:13,380
Well, try a little harder,
Janice, the defence does.

505
00:30:18,100 --> 00:30:19,970
Dr Graceman, Judge.

506
00:30:20,620 --> 00:30:23,210
Your instincts were right.

507
00:30:24,900 --> 00:30:28,610
I can't categorically say Mr Barron was murdered,
Sir John.

508
00:30:28,700 --> 00:30:30,690
But I can't rule it out.

509
00:30:31,220 --> 00:30:32,890
What can you say?

510
00:30:33,780 --> 00:30:38,610
Mr Barron had water in his lungs
before he went into the Thames.

511
00:30:41,180 --> 00:30:43,690
- He was dead before he went in?
- No.

512
00:30:44,700 --> 00:30:47,490
But I'd say he had been held underwater.

513
00:30:48,740 --> 00:30:51,970
He had drops of tap water in his lungs, Sir John.

514
00:30:53,020 --> 00:30:56,980
In near death, the lungs shut
down, trapping pockets of air

515
00:30:57,060 --> 00:31:00,090
and, in this case, tiny droplets of tap water.

516
00:31:02,340 --> 00:31:04,610
How can you tell it's tap water?

517
00:31:04,700 --> 00:31:07,570
Because of the diatoms present in the water.

518
00:31:08,260 --> 00:31:10,250
Tiny single-cell plants.

519
00:31:11,220 --> 00:31:13,780
These are consistent with tap water.

520
00:31:15,780 --> 00:31:20,250
So, are you saying that Mr Barron was brought to
near death first?

521
00:31:20,860 --> 00:31:22,130
It's possible.

522
00:31:22,220 --> 00:31:24,570
The alcohol in his blood went into the body

523
00:31:24,660 --> 00:31:27,690
between the tap water and Thames water.

524
00:31:27,780 --> 00:31:30,570
There were spots of alcohol in his lungs.

525
00:31:32,740 --> 00:31:34,450
And this tells us?

526
00:31:35,140 --> 00:31:37,570
Alcohol was probably poured into him.

527
00:31:37,660 --> 00:31:40,120
Bruising on the lower lip suggests as much.

528
00:31:40,220 --> 00:31:43,290
How could the first pathologist miss all this?

529
00:31:44,380 --> 00:31:46,330
It's easily done, Sir John.

530
00:31:46,420 --> 00:31:48,570
He was looking at a suicide.

531
00:31:49,100 --> 00:31:51,820
The second pathologist looks with more purpose.

532
00:31:51,900 --> 00:31:53,050
Mmm.

533
00:31:54,620 --> 00:31:57,290
I should need to show this to
the police and the coroner.

534
00:31:57,380 --> 00:31:59,050
May I keep this?

535
00:32:01,420 --> 00:32:04,970
I've known pathologists' reports condemn people
for murder on less.

536
00:32:05,060 --> 00:32:07,520
You should leave it to the police now, Dad.

537
00:32:07,620 --> 00:32:10,180
Have your mum and Haughton got at you?

538
00:32:10,260 --> 00:32:13,010
I thought you were keen to
show what happened to Rufus.

539
00:32:13,100 --> 00:32:15,050
Look, it's not our job.

540
00:32:15,660 --> 00:32:18,120
We can't just let this drop, Chas.

541
00:32:20,260 --> 00:32:22,930
If this gets too personal,
Mum will never forgive us.

542
00:32:23,020 --> 00:32:25,580
Look, he was probably murdered.

543
00:32:26,380 --> 00:32:28,970
And I'm going to insist on a murder enquiry.

544
00:32:31,140 --> 00:32:34,050
You do realise what this could mean?

545
00:32:34,420 --> 00:32:36,410
What you'd be bringing down on your head?

546
00:32:36,500 --> 00:32:38,210
I have a hard head.

547
00:32:39,860 --> 00:32:42,010
I'm not sure I can raise this in committee.

548
00:32:42,100 --> 00:32:44,410
Rufus Barron's evidence was key.

549
00:32:44,780 --> 00:32:46,490
I can't implicate the Home Secretary.

550
00:32:46,580 --> 00:32:48,970
Well, I'm prepared to think the unthinkable.

551
00:32:49,060 --> 00:32:52,130
The committee could question me as a witness
on Barron's evidence.

552
00:32:52,220 --> 00:32:54,450
- You're prepared to name names?
- Yeah.

553
00:32:54,540 --> 00:32:58,970
I'll just say what he said in court.
Haughton is tied up with Listfield.

554
00:32:59,500 --> 00:33:01,960
The result could mean we'll all be out of a job,
Sir John.

555
00:33:02,060 --> 00:33:03,540
Uh-huh.

556
00:33:05,100 --> 00:33:08,210
Somebody told me that you
were fearless, Mrs Kidman.

557
00:33:43,260 --> 00:33:45,770
- Smells good.
- Me or the food?

558
00:33:46,940 --> 00:33:48,210
Good shot.

559
00:33:49,740 --> 00:33:51,890
You know what happens, John?

560
00:33:52,300 --> 00:33:54,170
You call and I come running.

561
00:33:54,260 --> 00:33:56,130
That's all right because I like running.

562
00:33:56,220 --> 00:33:59,450
But what isn't all right is that you expect me to.

563
00:33:59,540 --> 00:34:01,690
- Call me and I'd come running.
- Yeah.

564
00:34:01,780 --> 00:34:03,180
Then I'd have to cook.

565
00:34:10,780 --> 00:34:13,140
You know what'll happen if you
don't take Charlie's advice?

566
00:34:13,145 --> 00:34:14,010
Yeah.

567
00:34:14,140 --> 00:34:17,050
Listfield, or Haughton, or hopefully both of them,
will go to jail.

568
00:34:17,140 --> 00:34:19,890
Hmm! If only things were that simple.

569
00:34:20,940 --> 00:34:25,170
- Maybe they are in your world.
- Where is this "my world" place, eh?

570
00:34:25,260 --> 00:34:27,530
Where you do things regardless.

571
00:34:28,540 --> 00:34:31,460
You mean like ignoring the fact that
I shouldn't be having a relationship

572
00:34:31,465 --> 00:34:34,575
with a member of the female bar who's before me?

573
00:34:34,820 --> 00:34:38,130
It would be something just to come
here and have a good time, instead of

574
00:34:38,220 --> 00:34:41,330
worrying whether you're going to be tipped off
a bridge some bright morning.

575
00:34:41,420 --> 00:34:42,930
- It's not going to happen.
- It might

576
00:34:43,020 --> 00:34:46,090
if Listfield is implicated, like you think.

577
00:34:46,180 --> 00:34:47,220
What do you want me to do?

578
00:34:47,300 --> 00:34:50,170
We should examine the evidence objectively

579
00:34:50,260 --> 00:34:52,010
and see how far we can push these two.

580
00:34:52,100 --> 00:34:54,580
Oh, then I should worry about you being pushed
off the bridge.

581
00:34:54,585 --> 00:34:57,855
Well, then we'd better make
sure there is evidence.

582
00:35:01,140 --> 00:35:04,570
Good evening, Sir Tim. Your
guest has already arrived.

583
00:35:08,100 --> 00:35:11,850
Neil, all the evidence
against us died with Barron.

584
00:35:13,580 --> 00:35:16,850
The second autopsy report
suggests he was murdered.

585
00:35:22,100 --> 00:35:25,490
Mrs Mills is now asking the
CPS to look at the evidence.

586
00:35:25,580 --> 00:35:27,410
He committed suicide.

587
00:35:29,300 --> 00:35:32,610
Can we be absolutely, absolutely
sure there's nothing.

588
00:35:32,700 --> 00:35:34,970
Barron might have left behind?

589
00:35:37,300 --> 00:35:40,890
If there is and Deed and Mills keep on pushing...

590
00:35:40,980 --> 00:35:42,300
If this was Russia...

591
00:35:42,380 --> 00:35:44,330
Good God, Tim, what are you saying to me?

592
00:35:44,420 --> 00:35:46,370
Has it come to that?

593
00:35:49,860 --> 00:35:53,330
Neil, there is no one who can incriminate you.

594
00:35:54,420 --> 00:35:55,770
Only me.

595
00:35:56,460 --> 00:35:58,890
And why would I want to do that?

596
00:36:03,540 --> 00:36:06,380
The Public Accounts Committee have
put me on notice to answer questions

597
00:36:06,385 --> 00:36:08,215
about the overspend.

598
00:36:09,260 --> 00:36:11,490
They want to talk to you as well.

599
00:36:11,580 --> 00:36:13,490
It'll go nowhere.

600
00:36:14,220 --> 00:36:15,650
Nowhere.

601
00:36:18,300 --> 00:36:21,130
With your faith, you should
be a Methodist minister.

602
00:36:23,820 --> 00:36:25,170
Let's eat.

603
00:36:28,060 --> 00:36:31,730
Come on, then. Oh, come on!

604
00:36:32,900 --> 00:36:34,890
Hey, how are you? Hey.

605
00:36:35,940 --> 00:36:36,940
How are you, huh?

606
00:36:38,300 --> 00:36:39,570
What sort of dog is she?

607
00:36:39,660 --> 00:36:41,770
A very friendly one.

608
00:36:43,420 --> 00:36:45,850
That's Mrs Mills' dog, isn't it?

609
00:36:47,060 --> 00:36:49,850
- Do I know you?
- No, but I know Mrs Mills,

610
00:36:50,420 --> 00:36:52,530
and Michael.

611
00:36:52,620 --> 00:36:54,570
And now I know your dog.

612
00:36:56,620 --> 00:36:58,570
- Who are you?
- Who, me?

613
00:37:00,820 --> 00:37:02,970
I hurt people for a living.

614
00:37:03,060 --> 00:37:04,850
I'll remember you.

615
00:37:10,900 --> 00:37:13,210
Judge, is everything all right?

616
00:37:16,380 --> 00:37:17,810
Yes. Yes, yes, yes.

617
00:37:18,460 --> 00:37:22,210
Well, why didn't you go to the police?
They'd give you protection.

618
00:37:24,100 --> 00:37:26,530
He said he knew you and Michael.

619
00:37:28,140 --> 00:37:31,770
A big man, grey hair and a moustache,
and cravat.

620
00:37:38,460 --> 00:37:41,290
Is this the man who kidnapped you?

621
00:37:41,380 --> 00:37:43,890
John, I'm scared. Go to the police.

622
00:37:49,180 --> 00:37:50,400
Please.

623
00:37:52,060 --> 00:37:55,730
Turning Charlie against Neil is so cruel.
She's very fond of him.

624
00:37:55,820 --> 00:37:56,850
He's such a decent man.

625
00:37:58,020 --> 00:37:59,980
I can't believe you don't know what you're doing!

626
00:37:59,985 --> 00:38:01,690
Oh, I know exactly what I'm doing.

627
00:38:01,780 --> 00:38:04,450
If you don't stop this, you'll end up without
a friend anywhere,

628
00:38:04,540 --> 00:38:07,050
apart from Jo Mills. She's
becoming as obsessed as you are.

629
00:38:07,140 --> 00:38:10,770
You are so wrong about Neil Haughton,
you'll see when we bring him down.

630
00:38:10,860 --> 00:38:14,370
You're just prejudiced because
he's more successful than you.

631
00:38:14,460 --> 00:38:16,170
Richer, younger, more powerful.

632
00:38:16,260 --> 00:38:18,210
- More corrupt.
- John,

633
00:38:18,300 --> 00:38:21,410
if you go to that committee and
name Neil without evidence,

634
00:38:21,500 --> 00:38:23,570
I will throttle you.

635
00:38:23,660 --> 00:38:25,850
- And if I have evidence?
- Oh!

636
00:38:26,820 --> 00:38:28,970
Driving me mad! Mad!

637
00:38:34,180 --> 00:38:35,400
What?

638
00:38:36,180 --> 00:38:38,820
Take a deep breath before you drive.

639
00:38:40,380 --> 00:38:41,380
Go to hell!

640
00:38:41,620 --> 00:38:43,290
How can he have evidence?

641
00:38:43,380 --> 00:38:46,930
I'm sorry. It's almost like
I'm responsible for him.

642
00:38:48,300 --> 00:38:50,130
George. That's silly.

643
00:38:51,180 --> 00:38:54,010
I doubt if anything will stop the Deed now.

644
00:38:54,100 --> 00:38:55,450
I know him.

645
00:38:55,540 --> 00:38:57,690
Maybe I should resign. Have done with it all.

646
00:38:57,780 --> 00:38:59,770
- Perhaps then he'll leave us alone.
- No.

647
00:38:59,860 --> 00:39:01,650
That means he wins.

648
00:39:02,100 --> 00:39:05,770
I'm beginning to think this
is not just about me, George.

649
00:39:07,100 --> 00:39:09,050
He wants to bring down the government.

650
00:39:09,300 --> 00:39:12,570
Mr Shoko, why did you agree
to take part in The Dungeon?

651
00:39:13,740 --> 00:39:15,010
For money.

652
00:39:15,100 --> 00:39:17,850
The money I could win would keep
my entire village in Malawi

653
00:39:17,940 --> 00:39:21,850
for generations to come.
- How much were you hoping to win?

654
00:39:21,940 --> 00:39:23,450
£500,000.

655
00:39:24,500 --> 00:39:27,650
- More from newspapers.
- What do you think now?

656
00:39:29,740 --> 00:39:31,770
I am deeply ashamed.

657
00:39:34,620 --> 00:39:36,730
Are you the breadwinner in your family?

658
00:39:36,820 --> 00:39:40,410
Yes. But my father and two
brothers also work back in Malawi.

659
00:39:40,500 --> 00:39:42,370
How much do they earn?

660
00:39:42,460 --> 00:39:44,530
About $2 a month.

661
00:39:45,380 --> 00:39:49,490
I was sent here with my brother to
work so I can send money back home.

662
00:39:49,580 --> 00:39:51,450
Now you're in prison?

663
00:39:52,300 --> 00:39:53,520
Yes.

664
00:39:53,620 --> 00:39:56,010
Now I don't send money home.

665
00:39:56,100 --> 00:39:58,970
My brothers and sisters can't go to school.

666
00:39:59,660 --> 00:40:01,370
- I see.
- Can you tell us what happened

667
00:40:01,460 --> 00:40:04,930
in The Dungeon that led to Reggie Ross's death?

668
00:40:05,180 --> 00:40:07,930
Two days before, we'd been acting up,

669
00:40:08,020 --> 00:40:11,130
so they decided to punish us.
- Who are "they"?

670
00:40:12,980 --> 00:40:14,570
The voices.

671
00:40:15,500 --> 00:40:17,370
They ran The Dungeon.

672
00:40:18,460 --> 00:40:20,690
What were the punishments?

673
00:40:22,100 --> 00:40:25,010
Because we weren't being entertaining,

674
00:40:26,740 --> 00:40:29,010
they made us clean everything.

675
00:40:29,540 --> 00:40:32,050
They put big mouth in charge, Liza.

676
00:40:32,660 --> 00:40:36,050
- She chose Butch Cassidy to help her.
- Did you take orders from them?

677
00:40:36,140 --> 00:40:38,330
I wouldn't. Not from them.

678
00:40:39,620 --> 00:40:42,370
- Not at first.
- What made you change your mind?

679
00:40:42,460 --> 00:40:46,170
They threatened to remove me,
and I wouldn't get the money.

680
00:40:48,340 --> 00:40:53,250
She gave me more and more orders, crazy things.

681
00:40:55,060 --> 00:40:58,370
She told me to scrub the
toilet with my toothbrush.

682
00:40:59,820 --> 00:41:01,690
I wouldn't,

683
00:41:01,780 --> 00:41:04,930
so they called me names, vile names.

684
00:41:05,020 --> 00:41:08,930
Did you have the opportunity to
leave at any point, Mr Shoko?

685
00:41:09,380 --> 00:41:12,570
If I left, no money.

686
00:41:15,820 --> 00:41:18,050
What happened when you
refused Liza Kelly's orders?

687
00:41:18,140 --> 00:41:20,170
Everybody was punished.

688
00:41:20,260 --> 00:41:22,650
They took away my Bible.

689
00:41:22,740 --> 00:41:24,570
We were all put on lock down.

690
00:41:24,900 --> 00:41:28,410
No booze, then no food, then no electricity.

691
00:41:29,540 --> 00:41:32,180
People got angry and blamed me.

692
00:41:32,820 --> 00:41:35,730
Then the queer said his queer friend
was getting my younger brother.

693
00:41:35,820 --> 00:41:37,930
I couldn't take that.

694
00:41:38,020 --> 00:41:39,610
We had a fight.

695
00:41:39,700 --> 00:41:43,210
Were you promised a light
sentence if you gave evidence?

696
00:41:43,660 --> 00:41:46,690
I was sentenced to three years!

697
00:41:47,300 --> 00:41:49,370
Why am I in prison and not them?

698
00:41:49,460 --> 00:41:52,890
What do you think of taking orders from women?

699
00:41:54,180 --> 00:41:57,170
- I don't take orders from that woman.
- But you had to,

700
00:41:57,260 --> 00:41:59,490
in order to stay in the game.

701
00:42:00,900 --> 00:42:03,010
You think women are inferior.

702
00:42:05,820 --> 00:42:08,850
Liza Kelly, especially inferior.

703
00:42:09,740 --> 00:42:13,290
I don't take orders from that woman.

704
00:42:13,820 --> 00:42:16,130
What about the gay man?

705
00:42:16,220 --> 00:42:18,250
He made you do things, too.

706
00:42:18,340 --> 00:42:20,130
He did what she said.

707
00:42:20,220 --> 00:42:24,570
Did you say "All gay men are degenerates"?

708
00:42:28,140 --> 00:42:29,810
- I don't remember.
- Ah.

709
00:42:31,020 --> 00:42:33,410
Then let me refresh your memory.

710
00:42:33,940 --> 00:42:35,340
Bundle C.

711
00:42:39,780 --> 00:42:41,370
Item 11.

712
00:42:43,060 --> 00:42:46,490
Part of the transcript of
what was said in The Dungeon.

713
00:42:50,860 --> 00:42:55,770
"Queers should be sent to Gehenna.
- It's an abomination."

714
00:42:59,220 --> 00:43:03,130
Was this your response to Butch
Cassidy giving you an order?

715
00:43:04,140 --> 00:43:05,970
It is not God's will.

716
00:43:06,940 --> 00:43:08,730
They are anti-life.

717
00:43:08,820 --> 00:43:11,970
- The Bible is clear on this.
- Ah, yes.

718
00:43:12,060 --> 00:43:15,530
It says on your application form to The Dungeon

719
00:43:15,620 --> 00:43:18,410
you're a born-again Christian?
- Yes.

720
00:43:18,500 --> 00:43:22,330
Indeed, we've seen you praying
and reading your Bible.

721
00:43:22,860 --> 00:43:28,810
Why would you, a devout Christian,
take part in this ritual humiliation?

722
00:43:31,140 --> 00:43:33,170
I did it to help my family.

723
00:43:34,180 --> 00:43:38,290
Even though you knew you'd be asked to do things
that were degrading,

724
00:43:38,380 --> 00:43:40,530
that, perhaps, were un-Christian,

725
00:43:40,620 --> 00:43:45,610
that you knew were calculated to make you angry.

726
00:43:45,700 --> 00:43:46,970
- My lord.
- Yes.

727
00:43:47,060 --> 00:43:49,370
Is this a speech or a question?

728
00:43:51,180 --> 00:43:55,250
The people in The Dungeon
called you thick, did they not?

729
00:43:55,340 --> 00:43:56,530
I am not thick.

730
00:43:56,620 --> 00:43:59,260
Did the other inmates call you thick?

731
00:43:59,340 --> 00:44:02,330
I am not thick!

732
00:44:02,420 --> 00:44:03,900
Liza gave you menial tasks

733
00:44:03,980 --> 00:44:07,490
because she said you weren't
bright enough to wash up.

734
00:44:07,940 --> 00:44:10,690
You went out of control because you couldn't bear
to be put down

735
00:44:10,780 --> 00:44:14,210
by a woman and a gay man.
- She can't say that!

736
00:44:21,180 --> 00:44:24,570
You've got quite a temper, Mr Shoko, haven't you?

737
00:44:26,940 --> 00:44:29,330
Your exploding and attacking Liza Kelly

738
00:44:29,420 --> 00:44:32,170
had nothing to do with Mr Storidge, did it?

739
00:44:32,260 --> 00:44:34,900
He, in fact, gave you several warnings, five.

740
00:44:34,980 --> 00:44:37,730
If you didn't cool it, you'd be out.

741
00:44:37,820 --> 00:44:40,210
She tried to make me look stupid.

742
00:44:40,780 --> 00:44:44,170
- I am not stupid.
- Mr Shoko,

743
00:44:44,860 --> 00:44:48,570
you knew the rules, but you chose to break them,
didn't you?

744
00:44:51,820 --> 00:44:54,650
Sir Ian Rochester is waiting in your room, Judge.

745
00:44:54,740 --> 00:44:55,890
Is he?

746
00:44:59,500 --> 00:45:01,330
Did we have a meeting?

747
00:45:01,940 --> 00:45:05,530
The Lord Chancellor has received a complaint
from the Home Office.

748
00:45:05,620 --> 00:45:09,890
You've asked a coroner to reconsider his findings
on the death of Rufus Barron.

749
00:45:09,980 --> 00:45:11,890
It needs re-examining.

750
00:45:11,980 --> 00:45:14,570
The CPS don't seem to think
there is sufficient evidence.

751
00:45:14,660 --> 00:45:17,890
Won't know until there's a
proper investigation, will we?

752
00:45:17,980 --> 00:45:20,100
The coroners' courts are not
within our jurisdiction.

753
00:45:20,105 --> 00:45:21,530
It's a matter for the Home Office.

754
00:45:21,620 --> 00:45:24,650
They are refusing to pay for a second autopsy.

755
00:45:26,380 --> 00:45:31,010
Okay, I'll pay for it.
Here, take my credit card. Have it!

756
00:45:33,660 --> 00:45:36,810
I will have that report on record.
And if suicide is discounted,

757
00:45:36,900 --> 00:45:39,210
there will be a murder enquiry.

758
00:45:58,180 --> 00:46:00,610
Sir John, Eric Little.

759
00:46:01,620 --> 00:46:03,890
Shall we talk across the road?

760
00:46:07,620 --> 00:46:11,530
As coroner, I gave the verdict
as I saw it, Sir John.

761
00:46:12,300 --> 00:46:13,620
Suicide.

762
00:46:15,380 --> 00:46:18,970
I'm convinced that there is
more to Rufus Barron's death.

763
00:46:19,900 --> 00:46:23,940
If you'd approached me first,
I could have asked for another autopsy.

764
00:46:24,020 --> 00:46:27,050
There would have been no
issue with the Home Office.

765
00:46:27,140 --> 00:46:29,930
Yes, I'm sorry, I should have approached you.

766
00:46:31,780 --> 00:46:35,290
Because of that, the Home
Office is being bloody-minded.

767
00:46:37,500 --> 00:46:38,930
Rufus Barron

768
00:46:40,660 --> 00:46:45,090
had been helping me uncover
possible corruption within the DTI.

769
00:46:46,700 --> 00:46:49,160
I didn't want to risk it getting back to them.

770
00:46:49,260 --> 00:46:53,530
Surely you're not suggesting that
someone there killed him, Sir John?

771
00:46:55,060 --> 00:46:58,810
That's what a murder enquiry will tell us.

772
00:47:03,980 --> 00:47:06,210
I will order another inquest.

773
00:47:08,340 --> 00:47:10,370
There will be resistance.

774
00:47:13,180 --> 00:47:15,970
With your leave, my lord, I'd
like to call Dr Petra Seal.

775
00:47:16,060 --> 00:47:18,490
Dr Seal is a behavioural psychologist

776
00:47:18,580 --> 00:47:22,460
employed by Hamling Productions to screen
and vet contestants.

777
00:47:24,100 --> 00:47:26,090
Dr Seal, can you tell the court the criteria

778
00:47:26,180 --> 00:47:28,640
for participating in The Dungeon?

779
00:47:28,740 --> 00:47:32,210
We were looking for people who were argumentative,
emotionally volatile,

780
00:47:32,300 --> 00:47:33,650
psychologically damaged.

781
00:47:33,740 --> 00:47:36,650
Did you have any concerns about
such people under stress?

782
00:47:36,740 --> 00:47:38,450
A great many.

783
00:47:38,540 --> 00:47:41,530
I was assured that the producers
had complete control.

784
00:47:41,620 --> 00:47:42,900
They could shut down The Dungeon

785
00:47:42,905 --> 00:47:45,210
and isolate any danger at a moment's warning.

786
00:47:45,300 --> 00:47:46,460
What do you think went wrong?

787
00:47:46,465 --> 00:47:49,815
My lord, is it for this
witness to speculate on that?

788
00:47:50,300 --> 00:47:51,700
She's an expert witness.

789
00:47:51,780 --> 00:47:53,290
Expert in TV production?

790
00:47:53,380 --> 00:47:56,210
She has been involved in the
last six Dungeon productions.

791
00:47:56,300 --> 00:47:59,090
Yes, I will allow her to answer,
providing the answer comes

792
00:47:59,180 --> 00:48:02,130
from her specialist knowledge of psychology.

793
00:48:03,300 --> 00:48:07,010
I think the producer and director
became mesmerised by the row.

794
00:48:07,100 --> 00:48:09,820
Despite assuring you they could control things?

795
00:48:09,900 --> 00:48:13,570
They were totally caught up in what
it could deliver, a large audience.

796
00:48:13,660 --> 00:48:16,650
Indeed, the previous row had
lifted audience figures.

797
00:48:16,740 --> 00:48:19,970
The more aggressive the inmates, the more the show
got talked about,

798
00:48:20,060 --> 00:48:22,490
the more people would tune in.

799
00:48:22,580 --> 00:48:25,410
That created an enormous pressure on them.

800
00:48:26,180 --> 00:48:30,090
Would it be fair to say they intended
letting the situation get out of hand?

801
00:48:30,180 --> 00:48:34,610
It would be fair to say that sounds
like a leading question, Mrs Mills.

802
00:48:35,060 --> 00:48:37,170
They wanted sparks to fly.

803
00:48:37,260 --> 00:48:39,570
- Did the sparks set fires?
- Yes.

804
00:48:40,540 --> 00:48:42,290
And fires get out of hand.

805
00:48:42,380 --> 00:48:45,250
How obvious would that have been?

806
00:48:45,340 --> 00:48:47,170
They're intelligent adults.

807
00:48:47,260 --> 00:48:51,410
They allowed unstable people.
- to run dangerously out of control.

808
00:48:51,500 --> 00:48:53,610
How was this characterised?

809
00:48:54,660 --> 00:48:56,890
Mr Shoko was the most vulnerable.

810
00:48:56,980 --> 00:49:00,650
He was of low intelligence, but
with a powerful self-image.

811
00:49:01,380 --> 00:49:05,290
He had no mechanism for dealing with sexual
or intellectual affronts.

812
00:49:05,380 --> 00:49:10,290
Yet, he was put in a situation
where a strident homosexual

813
00:49:10,380 --> 00:49:14,260
and an aggressive woman were told
to give him demeaning tasks.

814
00:49:15,220 --> 00:49:18,330
- It was a very volatile situation.

815
00:49:18,420 --> 00:49:20,650
Did the producer or the director know this?

816
00:49:20,740 --> 00:49:24,810
- I pointed it out to both of them.
- And what was their response?

817
00:49:24,900 --> 00:49:27,210
Mr Hodgekinson said, "No worries".

818
00:49:28,260 --> 00:49:30,410
Mr Storidge said, "Ditto".

819
00:49:31,660 --> 00:49:36,170
Did you observe either of their attitudes
contributing to this tragedy?

820
00:49:36,260 --> 00:49:38,130
Mr Hodgekinson is paranoid.

821
00:49:38,900 --> 00:49:43,610
He may hold down a responsible job,
but he's aggressive, suspicious.

822
00:49:44,380 --> 00:49:47,370
He believes people are trying to put him down.

823
00:49:49,260 --> 00:49:50,970
Mr Storidge, well,

824
00:49:51,540 --> 00:49:53,730
he just does what he's told.

825
00:49:53,820 --> 00:49:55,610
Thank you, Dr Seal.

826
00:50:00,580 --> 00:50:05,970
You say Mr Hodgekinson is
aggressive and suspicious.

827
00:50:06,060 --> 00:50:10,130
Yes. It's all made worse by his cocaine habit.

828
00:50:12,260 --> 00:50:14,050
Right. Stop there.

829
00:50:14,940 --> 00:50:17,010
Is there evidence of this?

830
00:50:17,780 --> 00:50:19,930
Only what I've seen.

831
00:50:20,020 --> 00:50:22,410
Cocaine exacerbates the paranoia?

832
00:50:23,820 --> 00:50:26,050
- It certainly does.
- Would it have any effect...

833
00:50:26,140 --> 00:50:27,650
Mr Hodgekinson!

834
00:50:28,620 --> 00:50:30,170
Just calm down.

835
00:50:30,860 --> 00:50:33,420
Would it have any effect

836
00:50:33,500 --> 00:50:37,770
on his ability to assess fast-moving situations?

837
00:50:38,340 --> 00:50:40,690
It never improves judgment.

838
00:50:40,780 --> 00:50:41,930
My lord, uh...

839
00:50:43,420 --> 00:50:47,690
May I have a short adjournment to
take instructions from my client?

840
00:50:47,780 --> 00:50:50,850
Yes, I think we should. Tell
my clerk when you're ready.

841
00:50:51,740 --> 00:50:54,490
Practically everyone in
television uses coke, Newman.

842
00:50:54,580 --> 00:50:56,690
To argue that is only to incriminate you!

843
00:50:56,780 --> 00:51:01,370
How can that psychologist accuse me?
I've seen her using it, and the execs.

844
00:51:01,460 --> 00:51:05,530
If my judgment was impaired, so was theirs!

845
00:51:06,180 --> 00:51:10,930
Dr Seal, are you rational in
reasoning, of sound judgment?

846
00:51:12,460 --> 00:51:14,290
I'd like to think so.

847
00:51:15,420 --> 00:51:18,850
Despite your large and persistent coke habit?

848
00:51:23,660 --> 00:51:28,010
Dr Seal, you needn't answer that if
you feel it might incriminate you.

849
00:51:28,900 --> 00:51:30,850
Well, Dr Seal?

850
00:51:35,020 --> 00:51:38,690
Or do you only have the moral courage to accuse
others of doing

851
00:51:38,780 --> 00:51:40,570
what you do yourself?

852
00:51:44,740 --> 00:51:45,890
Charlie is furious at me.

853
00:51:45,980 --> 00:51:49,570
Well, does that mean you might change your mind
about Haughton?

854
00:51:49,660 --> 00:51:51,330
She'll come round.

855
00:51:52,020 --> 00:51:54,450
Maybe she's scared for you, John.

856
00:51:55,140 --> 00:51:56,540
I'm scared.

857
00:51:58,660 --> 00:52:02,050
Motive is all that connects
Listfield to Barron's murder.

858
00:52:02,140 --> 00:52:04,250
- There is no evidence.
- Yet.

859
00:52:04,660 --> 00:52:07,810
- The police must investigate.
- Yeah, but will they?

860
00:52:07,900 --> 00:52:10,220
Well, maybe we'll get something
from the Select Committee.

861
00:52:10,225 --> 00:52:14,370
No, John, the bribery allegation is a
separate issue from Barron's murder.

862
00:52:14,700 --> 00:52:17,450
One might lead to the other, eventually.

863
00:52:25,020 --> 00:52:28,410
- They're ready for you, Judge.
- Are the same press in?

864
00:52:28,500 --> 00:52:29,930
Oh, they are.

865
00:52:30,020 --> 00:52:32,740
Good. Put some dock officers on the doors.

866
00:52:33,900 --> 00:52:35,250
Yes, Judge.

867
00:52:35,900 --> 00:52:37,530
All rise.

868
00:52:45,100 --> 00:52:47,660
Will members of the press please stand up?

869
00:52:50,180 --> 00:52:55,200
Will anyone who has a digital phone-camera
please show it, now.

870
00:53:02,820 --> 00:53:06,050
Usher, will you collect them, please, label them,

871
00:53:06,580 --> 00:53:10,770
and ask the owners to identify them afterwards?
Mr Clerk, thank you.

872
00:53:20,340 --> 00:53:23,650
Any of you that has a camera, of any kind at all,

873
00:53:23,740 --> 00:53:26,170
that hasn't declared it, will be held in contempt.

874
00:53:26,260 --> 00:53:28,290
This is your last chance.

875
00:53:37,340 --> 00:53:39,170
One more down there.

876
00:53:44,300 --> 00:53:45,700
Right.

877
00:53:47,100 --> 00:53:49,930
Which of you took this photo of Dr Seal?

878
00:53:58,860 --> 00:54:01,850
Taking photos in court is a contempt.

879
00:54:04,740 --> 00:54:08,250
These pictures appeared in
five national newspapers

880
00:54:08,340 --> 00:54:10,530
downloaded from the Internet.

881
00:54:13,220 --> 00:54:17,810
Now, I think that there is a conspiracy here
to hold this court in contempt.

882
00:54:22,420 --> 00:54:24,250
Still nothing to say?

883
00:54:26,780 --> 00:54:30,370
Right, I'll hold all of you in the cells
and deal with this later.

884
00:54:30,460 --> 00:54:32,330
Dock officers, take them out.

885
00:54:45,340 --> 00:54:47,930
We must speak with Deed most urgently, Monty.

886
00:54:48,020 --> 00:54:50,130
The press are saying he's
completely out of control.

887
00:54:50,220 --> 00:54:52,650
Contempt of court cannot be tolerated, Ian.

888
00:54:52,740 --> 00:54:54,660
There are ways of dealing with it less likely to

889
00:54:54,740 --> 00:54:57,380
antagonise the media in election year.

890
00:54:58,460 --> 00:55:02,690
Are you being quite serious? The court is supreme.

891
00:55:15,380 --> 00:55:16,860
Thanks, Coop.

892
00:55:16,940 --> 00:55:19,500
I'm going to adjourn for the
rest of today and tomorrow.

893
00:55:19,580 --> 00:55:21,730
I hope this won't prove too inconvenient.

894
00:55:21,820 --> 00:55:24,850
I've been called to give evidence before
the Public Accounts Committee.

895
00:55:24,940 --> 00:55:28,210
It will give the prosecution time to reorganise
their witnesses.

896
00:55:28,300 --> 00:55:30,010
We feel quite organised, thank you.

897
00:55:30,100 --> 00:55:33,810
Then it will give the defence time to be just
as organised.

898
00:55:37,660 --> 00:55:40,850
Sir John, I believe a defendant in your court
made a statement

899
00:55:40,940 --> 00:55:44,850
about the £700 million overspend on the navy's
new radar system,

900
00:55:44,940 --> 00:55:46,770
one Rufus Barron.

901
00:55:46,860 --> 00:55:51,010
Yes, well, Mrs Recorder Mills was actually trying
the case,

902
00:55:51,100 --> 00:55:54,090
but I was present, I heard the allegation.

903
00:55:54,180 --> 00:55:56,610
Was this subsequently
repeated to you out of court?

904
00:55:56,700 --> 00:55:59,420
Yes, it was, with some conviction.

905
00:55:59,500 --> 00:56:02,250
- Did you believe Mr Barron?
- Yes, I did.

906
00:56:04,900 --> 00:56:08,210
What is the nature of the
allegation made by Mr Barron?

907
00:56:08,300 --> 00:56:12,650
That the contract overspend was due to double
and triple billing.

908
00:56:13,540 --> 00:56:17,010
What is that, Sir John?
Invoicing for the same goods?

909
00:56:17,780 --> 00:56:20,050
Yeah. See,

910
00:56:20,140 --> 00:56:23,250
say you have a box of screws costing £15.

911
00:56:24,980 --> 00:56:29,450
That same box of screws is invoiced a second
and a third time

912
00:56:29,540 --> 00:56:31,050
using different codes.

913
00:56:31,140 --> 00:56:34,970
So, there you go, that's £30 of taxpayers' money.

914
00:56:35,060 --> 00:56:38,210
And how can this be, with all the checks
along the way?

915
00:56:39,260 --> 00:56:42,730
I thought that was what you were here to find out.

916
00:56:42,820 --> 00:56:46,130
Yes, by questioning witnesses.

917
00:56:48,660 --> 00:56:52,250
Did Mr Barron name anyone responsible, Sir John?

918
00:56:52,500 --> 00:56:54,850
Yes. He said his boss,

919
00:56:54,940 --> 00:56:59,960
Sir Tim Listfield, bribed officials in the DTI,
including a minister,

920
00:57:00,060 --> 00:57:03,940
with £700,000 in cash to sign
off on the over-billing.

921
00:57:04,020 --> 00:57:07,210
Oh, is that the cash that Mr Barron was accused

922
00:57:07,300 --> 00:57:09,970
of forging Listfield's signature to obtain?

923
00:57:10,060 --> 00:57:12,700
Well, as I said, he was acquitted.

924
00:57:14,020 --> 00:57:16,050
So, is that why he committed suicide?

925
00:57:16,140 --> 00:57:18,600
No. I suspect that's why he was murdered.

926
00:57:20,780 --> 00:57:22,570
This is all only speculation.

927
00:57:23,900 --> 00:57:27,210
- We await the evidence.
- Do you have such evidence?

928
00:57:28,060 --> 00:57:30,250
That's not for this enquiry.

929
00:57:30,980 --> 00:57:35,130
Did Mr Barron name the minister
who was allegedly bribed?

930
00:57:39,500 --> 00:57:40,770
Sir John?

931
00:57:53,300 --> 00:57:54,410
Yes, he did.

932
00:57:54,500 --> 00:57:58,610
He named the then President of the Board of Trade,
Neil Haughton.

933
00:57:58,700 --> 00:58:01,530
He did it. Despite everything, he named me.

934
00:58:02,820 --> 00:58:04,330
You have yet to give evidence.

935
00:58:04,420 --> 00:58:06,420
This is no longer just about a bribery allegation.

936
00:58:06,460 --> 00:58:09,810
He is trying to connect me,
through you, to a murder.

937
00:58:09,900 --> 00:58:12,050
No one was murdered, Neil.

938
00:58:18,180 --> 00:58:21,410
Regrettably, Tim, I have to
disassociate myself from you,

939
00:58:21,500 --> 00:58:23,770
before the PM demands my resignation.

940
00:58:24,060 --> 00:58:27,210
Then I can no longer rely on political lobbying?

941
00:58:29,740 --> 00:58:34,250
Oh, well, alternative solutions are needed.

942
00:58:45,580 --> 00:58:46,850
Come in.

943
00:58:48,460 --> 00:58:51,210
Well, the press has given us a drubbing.

944
00:58:51,580 --> 00:58:53,810
Are we so out of control?

945
00:58:53,900 --> 00:58:56,970
Well, they're complaining
that we're not accountable.

946
00:58:57,860 --> 00:59:00,650
- We can't be sacked.
- Well, they're right.

947
00:59:01,660 --> 00:59:03,930
They've obviously got a friend on the inside.

948
00:59:04,020 --> 00:59:06,690
- I hope you don't think it's me.
- No.

949
00:59:06,780 --> 00:59:09,050
It's Rochester, unattributed.

950
00:59:09,780 --> 00:59:13,210
I should think the government have called in
a lot of favours on this one.

951
00:59:13,300 --> 00:59:15,730
I'm surprised they've got any due.

952
00:59:16,420 --> 00:59:19,060
- So, what do you plan to do?
- Nothing.

953
00:59:20,180 --> 00:59:23,290
No matter how bad the press, tomorrow they'll be
attacking somebody else,

954
00:59:23,380 --> 00:59:25,090
hopefully, Haughton.

955
00:59:26,220 --> 00:59:28,050
I wouldn't count on it.

956
00:59:39,420 --> 00:59:42,170
Ready to reveal who took the photographs?

957
00:59:45,860 --> 00:59:48,290
I suppose it's no hardship
spending a night in a cell,

958
00:59:48,380 --> 00:59:51,020
when you've been sent there by a fanatical judge

959
00:59:51,100 --> 00:59:52,930
who is out of control, eh?

960
00:59:53,020 --> 00:59:58,330
My lord, if I might put the argument
on behalf of my client, Mr Zand,

961
00:59:58,420 --> 01:00:01,250
in relation to the principles of a free press?

962
01:00:01,340 --> 01:00:04,290
Mr Palmer, the press is not absolutely free.

963
01:00:04,380 --> 01:00:07,970
It cannot, for example, without
incurring serious legal action,

964
01:00:08,060 --> 01:00:12,490
print lies or obscenities, nor
can it commit contempt of court.

965
01:00:12,980 --> 01:00:16,690
My lord, I would like to
address the court on Article 6

966
01:00:16,780 --> 01:00:20,850
of the European Convention and the
guaranteed right to a fair trial.

967
01:00:20,940 --> 01:00:22,810
I'm sure your client will get one.

968
01:00:22,900 --> 01:00:26,780
But there is no evidence that my client took
the offending photo.

969
01:00:26,860 --> 01:00:28,650
Is this your client?

970
01:00:29,140 --> 01:00:30,770
- This one, is it?
- Yes, that's him.

971
01:00:30,860 --> 01:00:33,370
Get your hands out of your pockets.

972
01:00:34,220 --> 01:00:35,290
Go on.

973
01:00:35,380 --> 01:00:39,890
My lord, there is no evidence that my client took
the offending photo.

974
01:00:39,980 --> 01:00:43,490
Papers publishing it off the
Internet and commenting on it

975
01:00:43,580 --> 01:00:45,170
is a legitimate pursuit.

976
01:00:45,260 --> 01:00:47,490
No, Mr Palmer, I have ruled.

977
01:00:47,580 --> 01:00:50,890
And as you know from your
newspaper, I am a despot.

978
01:00:50,980 --> 01:00:53,170
Dock officers, take them out.

979
01:01:04,660 --> 01:01:07,810
I can't let this happen, Keith.
What are you talking about, Roy?

980
01:01:07,900 --> 01:01:09,930
I'm saying, I can't go through with this.

981
01:01:10,020 --> 01:01:11,610
Don't be stupid.

982
01:01:11,700 --> 01:01:14,930
- Oh, I don't believe this.
- They're ready, Roy.

983
01:01:15,020 --> 01:01:16,380
- Yeah. Can we have a moment?
- No.

984
01:01:16,385 --> 01:01:19,105
- The Judge is waiting...
- Just a second.

985
01:01:20,580 --> 01:01:22,780
This is total career suicide.
You know that, don't you?

986
01:01:23,820 --> 01:01:27,050
- I no longer have a career.
- Are you mad?

987
01:01:27,140 --> 01:01:28,410
When you win this case,

988
01:01:28,500 --> 01:01:31,020
everyone is going to want you to
direct their shows. Everybody!

989
01:01:31,100 --> 01:01:34,290
- Roy, the judge is waiting.
- Please, Simon!

990
01:01:35,020 --> 01:01:37,480
I'll try for a short adjournment.

991
01:01:40,020 --> 01:01:43,210
Did someone put you up to
this?-Is this Adrian Hamling?

992
01:01:43,300 --> 01:01:46,450
- I feel guilty.
- We made that geezer millions.

993
01:01:46,540 --> 01:01:48,260
He's just trying to do us out of our share.

994
01:01:48,265 --> 01:01:51,535
- It was our fault.
- Your fault, not mine.

995
01:01:53,580 --> 01:01:57,050
- You knew what would happen.
- No, no, no, don't try and rope me

996
01:01:57,140 --> 01:01:59,130
into your tacky little guilt trip.

997
01:01:59,220 --> 01:02:02,410
If you want to publicly kill yourself,
do it on TV, make some money.

998
01:02:13,420 --> 01:02:14,900
Right.

999
01:02:16,420 --> 01:02:19,410
I drifted away from the Church at university,

1000
01:02:20,500 --> 01:02:23,140
but I still have my Christian beliefs.

1001
01:02:25,340 --> 01:02:27,370
Central to those is conscience.

1002
01:02:27,460 --> 01:02:31,090
And did this conscience allow you to set people
against each other

1003
01:02:31,180 --> 01:02:33,050
in the latest Dungeon TV show?

1004
01:02:33,140 --> 01:02:34,930
I'm sorry I did that.

1005
01:02:35,740 --> 01:02:38,730
At the time, it didn't matter
if people were belittled

1006
01:02:38,820 --> 01:02:43,210
or made fools of, just so long as the show reached
18 million viewers.

1007
01:02:44,420 --> 01:02:48,050
- How successful was the show?
- We didn't reach 18 million,

1008
01:02:49,220 --> 01:02:52,010
but we all stood to make a lot of money.

1009
01:02:53,260 --> 01:02:56,410
Were you promised a bonus for 18 million viewers?

1010
01:02:59,300 --> 01:03:00,700
A big one.

1011
01:03:00,780 --> 01:03:03,690
Well, how did you plan to reach that figure?

1012
01:03:03,780 --> 01:03:06,810
By getting the inmates at each other's throats.

1013
01:03:07,500 --> 01:03:10,690
I want you to think very carefully
about this, Mr Storidge.

1014
01:03:10,780 --> 01:03:13,530
Search your conscience before answering.

1015
01:03:15,620 --> 01:03:19,890
Did you or your colleagues anticipate
any of the inmates getting hurt?

1016
01:03:28,900 --> 01:03:31,090
I'm sorry, Keith, I can't.

1017
01:03:31,180 --> 01:03:33,370
- I don't care about my career.
- It's suicide.

1018
01:03:33,460 --> 01:03:36,530
Yes, the possibility of somebody
getting hurt was discussed.

1019
01:03:36,620 --> 01:03:39,010
Mrs Mills, just a moment.

1020
01:03:39,540 --> 01:03:41,330
Would the jury go out, please?

1021
01:03:41,420 --> 01:03:44,570
There is a technical matter I have to deal with.

1022
01:03:54,700 --> 01:03:57,970
Mr Storidge, are you now
intending to plead guilty?

1023
01:03:58,060 --> 01:03:59,380
Yes.

1024
01:03:59,460 --> 01:04:03,290
- This is insane!
- Mr Hodgekinson, your turn will come.

1025
01:04:03,860 --> 01:04:07,970
Members of the jury, Mr Storidge
has changed his plea to guilty.

1026
01:04:09,060 --> 01:04:11,410
He will be sentenced at a later date.

1027
01:04:11,500 --> 01:04:14,490
He is now giving evidence for the prosecution.

1028
01:04:14,940 --> 01:04:17,850
Did you discuss the possibility of
someone being injured in The Dungeon?

1029
01:04:17,940 --> 01:04:21,650
Yes, after the first fight, when the ratings rose.

1030
01:04:21,740 --> 01:04:24,140
We decided we could manipulate
them into fighting some more.

1031
01:04:24,145 --> 01:04:26,130
The more serious the fight, the better.

1032
01:04:26,220 --> 01:04:31,530
Mr Storidge, was the possibility of
anyone getting killed ever raised?

1033
01:04:34,660 --> 01:04:38,010
Dr Seal warned what could happen, with the sort of

1034
01:04:38,700 --> 01:04:41,160
personalities we were putting under such stress.

1035
01:04:41,260 --> 01:04:44,570
- Did anyone pay this any heed?
- We discussed "what if".

1036
01:04:44,660 --> 01:04:47,010
Were these serious discussions?

1037
01:04:48,220 --> 01:04:50,780
Keith asked the channel lawyers
what the consequences would be

1038
01:04:50,860 --> 01:04:52,730
if someone was killed.

1039
01:04:53,700 --> 01:04:55,690
What was their opinion?

1040
01:04:55,780 --> 01:04:58,730
I don't know, I didn't see the report.

1041
01:04:58,820 --> 01:05:01,570
But Keith said the company would
not be criminally liable,

1042
01:05:01,660 --> 01:05:04,890
provided no one who was a
directing mind gave instructions

1043
01:05:04,980 --> 01:05:07,090
which amounted to gross negligence.

1044
01:05:07,180 --> 01:05:10,810
Had there been a mind-directing
this action, Mr Storidge?

1045
01:05:15,620 --> 01:05:17,650
I have to take the blame.

1046
01:05:18,300 --> 01:05:19,780
- And Keith.
- Rubbish!

1047
01:05:19,860 --> 01:05:22,690
Part of the plan was to manipulate Tendai Shoko.

1048
01:05:22,780 --> 01:05:26,330
Butch said his homosexual friend had seduced
Tendai's little brother.

1049
01:05:26,420 --> 01:05:29,060
That's when he went berserk, and we knew he would.

1050
01:05:29,140 --> 01:05:30,570
- This was the first fight?
- Yes.

1051
01:05:30,660 --> 01:05:31,900
Why didn't you remove him then?

1052
01:05:33,420 --> 01:05:37,130
At the planning meeting the next day, we
saw what a boost it gave the ratings.

1053
01:05:37,220 --> 01:05:40,890
Can you tell the court who was at these
planning meetings?

1054
01:05:40,980 --> 01:05:44,010
Me, Keith, Petra Seal, the psychologist...

1055
01:05:44,100 --> 01:05:45,450
Slow down.

1056
01:05:46,060 --> 01:05:47,280
Petra Seal, yes.

1057
01:05:47,380 --> 01:05:51,730
Sometimes Adrian Hamling, Toby Ronson
from the channel sometimes,

1058
01:05:51,820 --> 01:05:54,540
and the director of programmes, Dominic Hughes.

1059
01:05:54,740 --> 01:06:00,370
Right. Did you discuss the effect on the ratings
of someone's death?

1060
01:06:00,460 --> 01:06:02,130
No, not really.

1061
01:06:02,780 --> 01:06:05,170
I mean, we joked about it. It...

1062
01:06:06,620 --> 01:06:09,050
It seemed quite funny at the time.

1063
01:06:10,420 --> 01:06:13,410
I can't say anyone was being really serious.

1064
01:06:14,380 --> 01:06:16,810
- Mrs Mills?
- Who first raised it?

1065
01:06:17,820 --> 01:06:19,650
I think it was Keith.

1066
01:06:23,020 --> 01:06:26,530
Did you recommend Tim Listfield for a knighthood?

1067
01:06:29,660 --> 01:06:32,570
- No, it was my department.
- Not you?

1068
01:06:32,660 --> 01:06:34,370
No. My department.

1069
01:06:34,980 --> 01:06:38,530
On a recommendation directed
by you, Home Secretary?

1070
01:06:41,900 --> 01:06:43,650
It came from the DTI.

1071
01:06:49,460 --> 01:06:51,010
Thank you.

1072
01:06:52,380 --> 01:06:55,930
Is that your memo to the
Permanent Secretary at the DTI?

1073
01:06:57,220 --> 01:06:58,250
It appears to be.

1074
01:06:58,340 --> 01:07:00,930
Recommending Tim Listfield for a gong?

1075
01:07:01,020 --> 01:07:04,570
- He is a leading industrialist.
- He's also a friend of yours.

1076
01:07:04,660 --> 01:07:06,850
We were friends a while ago.

1077
01:07:07,620 --> 01:07:10,410
Do you have any business interests with this man?

1078
01:07:10,500 --> 01:07:12,610
No. I knew him, that's all.

1079
01:07:18,780 --> 01:07:23,690
Why were you signing off on a defence contract,
not the Defence Secretary?

1080
01:07:29,580 --> 01:07:32,170
As I recall, the defence contract was started

1081
01:07:32,260 --> 01:07:34,900
before Sir Tim took over Whittakers,

1082
01:07:34,980 --> 01:07:36,930
the defence contractor.

1083
01:07:37,340 --> 01:07:39,900
But it was subsequent to that acquisition that
the contract

1084
01:07:39,980 --> 01:07:41,330
went over budget?

1085
01:07:50,540 --> 01:07:52,130
I don't know. As I recall,

1086
01:07:52,220 --> 01:07:55,370
the DTI, in fact, sold the radar equipment

1087
01:07:55,460 --> 01:07:59,240
to the Australian and Portuguese
Navies for a vast profit.

1088
01:07:59,340 --> 01:08:03,410
Is that why no one questioned
the overspend of £700 million?

1089
01:08:04,460 --> 01:08:08,170
As I recall, there were some
unforeseen difficulties

1090
01:08:08,260 --> 01:08:10,290
at the development stage.

1091
01:08:10,380 --> 01:08:12,840
Was this the start of your
relationship with Sir Tim?

1092
01:08:12,940 --> 01:08:15,400
No, I knew him before this.

1093
01:08:21,060 --> 01:08:23,700
- You knew him to be honest?
- Of course.

1094
01:08:26,300 --> 01:08:30,410
Home Secretary, you said you and Sir Tim
have no business interests.

1095
01:08:31,180 --> 01:08:33,820
No. I've said no, Mrs Kidman.

1096
01:08:34,260 --> 01:08:37,530
Were you not on the board of
his company called Mentor?

1097
01:08:37,620 --> 01:08:39,730
I resigned on becoming a minister.

1098
01:08:39,820 --> 01:08:41,570
What was the function of Mentor?

1099
01:08:41,660 --> 01:08:45,890
As I recall, a PR company
for science-based industry.

1100
01:08:45,980 --> 01:08:48,490
Did it lobby on behalf of
Listfield for the radar contract?

1101
01:08:48,580 --> 01:08:50,530
No. Definitely not.

1102
01:08:50,620 --> 01:08:52,690
Could you tell the committee, minister,

1103
01:08:52,780 --> 01:08:55,690
how profitable the radar contract has been?

1104
01:08:56,620 --> 01:09:00,090
It's made over £1 .4 billion to date.

1105
01:09:00,180 --> 01:09:02,770
- Is it still selling?
- Yes, of course.

1106
01:09:02,860 --> 01:09:04,850
The question we all want answered is,

1107
01:09:04,940 --> 01:09:08,850
did you receive a bribe of £700,000 from Sir Tim?

1108
01:09:11,260 --> 01:09:13,370
That's totally ludicrous.

1109
01:09:16,220 --> 01:09:19,370
- I hope this is finally the end.
- So do I, John.

1110
01:09:20,100 --> 01:09:21,320
George.

1111
01:09:25,900 --> 01:09:27,810
He has his supporters here.

1112
01:09:27,900 --> 01:09:31,250
Well, you'd hardly expect him
to roll over, would you?

1113
01:09:31,340 --> 01:09:32,660
The profitability of the contract

1114
01:09:32,665 --> 01:09:34,770
doesn't preclude his taking a bribe, you know.

1115
01:09:34,860 --> 01:09:36,930
No, of course it doesn't.

1116
01:09:37,860 --> 01:09:40,580
Nor does an uncorroborated allegation.

1117
01:09:49,220 --> 01:09:51,210
I've a feeling Haughton's gonna walk, you know.

1118
01:09:51,300 --> 01:09:52,620
He should go to prison.

1119
01:09:52,700 --> 01:09:56,370
- He was so obviously lying.
- It's what politicians do best.

1120
01:09:56,460 --> 01:10:00,570
They've still got to question Listfield.
- I am not going to give up.

1121
01:10:02,460 --> 01:10:04,580
You have the verdict of the
coroner's second inquest.

1122
01:10:04,585 --> 01:10:07,410
There really isn't enough in it for
me to interview the Home Secretary

1123
01:10:07,500 --> 01:10:10,140
or Sir Tim Listfield about Rufus Barron's death.

1124
01:10:10,220 --> 01:10:13,890
It casts substantial doubt
on his supposed suicide.

1125
01:10:18,420 --> 01:10:23,930
My lord, the Home Office has requested me to stop
this investigation.

1126
01:10:25,140 --> 01:10:28,920
Well, I strongly advise you
to ignore their request.

1127
01:10:29,140 --> 01:10:31,700
If you don't, then the government will feel free

1128
01:10:31,780 --> 01:10:34,610
to interfere in any investigation at will.

1129
01:10:35,700 --> 01:10:38,490
That is the beginning of a police state.

1130
01:10:39,820 --> 01:10:42,490
If the Chief Constable chooses to ignore
the Home Office's advice,

1131
01:10:42,580 --> 01:10:44,090
there's nothing I can do.

1132
01:10:44,180 --> 01:10:45,890
You were supposed to tell him.

1133
01:10:45,980 --> 01:10:47,970
I'm sorry, Tim. I'm late for a meeting.

1134
01:10:48,060 --> 01:10:52,100
- Deed and Mills are pushing this.
- Tim, please don't contact me.

1135
01:10:55,900 --> 01:10:58,170
Let's get it sorted, Steve.

1136
01:10:59,780 --> 01:11:05,570
Members of the jury, no one disputes that
Mr Shoko killed Reggie Ross.

1137
01:11:06,860 --> 01:11:09,610
The Crown accepts that he was so provoked,

1138
01:11:10,420 --> 01:11:14,810
any reasonable person of the
same general age and character

1139
01:11:15,460 --> 01:11:17,970
might have behaved as he did.

1140
01:11:19,700 --> 01:11:22,970
The question for you now,
is whether Mr Hodgekinson,

1141
01:11:23,060 --> 01:11:26,530
through his controlling the
programme as its producer

1142
01:11:27,020 --> 01:11:30,650
and his acquiescence in the
mounting tension and violence,

1143
01:11:30,740 --> 01:11:33,330
broke his duty of care to the inmates,

1144
01:11:34,620 --> 01:11:37,810
and did so in a way that was so negligent,

1145
01:11:37,900 --> 01:11:40,410
what the law terms gross negligence,

1146
01:11:41,020 --> 01:11:45,010
you think that his behaviour could be condemned
as criminal.

1147
01:11:46,940 --> 01:11:51,250
Now, Mr Hodgekinson says that
there was no negligence.

1148
01:11:52,860 --> 01:11:57,250
He says that the danger was
unforeseen and unforeseeable.

1149
01:11:59,340 --> 01:12:03,650
Mr Mason Allen says this was a television show.

1150
01:12:04,580 --> 01:12:08,690
Everybody in The Dungeon knew
precisely why they were there.

1151
01:12:08,780 --> 01:12:12,010
He also says that Mr Storidge was an unreliable

1152
01:12:12,100 --> 01:12:13,420
and an untruthful witness,

1153
01:12:13,500 --> 01:12:16,570
and that he was only there to save his own skin.

1154
01:12:17,460 --> 01:12:22,740
You can only find the defendant guilty
of manslaughter if you are sure

1155
01:12:22,820 --> 01:12:25,610
that death resulted from conduct of the defendant

1156
01:12:25,700 --> 01:12:28,210
that was criminally lacking in care.

1157
01:12:29,380 --> 01:12:35,690
Aand "criminal" is a standard to be set by you
as members of the jury.

1158
01:12:36,940 --> 01:12:42,220
How far conduct must depart from
acceptable standards to be characterised

1159
01:12:42,300 --> 01:12:46,080
as criminal, is necessarily a question of degree.

1160
01:12:47,500 --> 01:12:49,570
You must make the decision.

1161
01:12:53,980 --> 01:12:55,850
- Is the jury back already?
- No, Judge.

1162
01:12:55,940 --> 01:12:57,160
Mrs Kidman's office rang,

1163
01:12:57,260 --> 01:12:59,900
and they're tearing Sir Tim Listfield to shreds.

1164
01:12:59,980 --> 01:13:02,340
- She'd like you to be there.
- I thought that was tomorrow.

1165
01:13:02,345 --> 01:13:05,295
- Apparently not.
- Get the jury back.

1166
01:13:07,380 --> 01:13:10,690
Are you near to reaching a verdict
in respect to the defendant?

1167
01:13:10,780 --> 01:13:12,570
No, my lord, nothing like.

1168
01:13:12,660 --> 01:13:16,890
All right, then I'm going to
send you home for the night.

1169
01:13:16,980 --> 01:13:18,300
Thank you.

1170
01:13:29,780 --> 01:13:31,490
I'm at the court.

1171
01:13:35,060 --> 01:13:38,130
No, he hasn't taken his own car.
He will, sooner or later.

1172
01:13:38,220 --> 01:13:40,680
All right. Jo, are you all right?

1173
01:13:42,500 --> 01:13:44,130
- No.
- Jo?

1174
01:13:45,780 --> 01:13:48,420
Mr Chester, I have to see the judge.

1175
01:13:51,700 --> 01:13:54,530
- He's gone to the House of Commons.
- Oh.

1176
01:13:54,620 --> 01:13:56,300
I've just seen the man who threatened him.

1177
01:13:56,305 --> 01:13:58,130
- Shall I call the police?
- Yeah.

1178
01:13:58,220 --> 01:13:59,850
I'm gonna follow him.

1179
01:13:59,940 --> 01:14:02,450
Coop! I need John's car.

1180
01:14:41,740 --> 01:14:44,050
I thought you wanted no contact.

1181
01:14:44,620 --> 01:14:48,050
Sussex police want to interview
me about Rufus Barron.

1182
01:14:49,340 --> 01:14:51,800
They want to question you, too.

1183
01:14:51,900 --> 01:14:54,290
Fine. For you, maybe.

1184
01:14:56,420 --> 01:14:58,820
With the publicity surrounding
the Select Committee enquiry,

1185
01:14:58,825 --> 01:15:00,610
and now this,

1186
01:15:00,700 --> 01:15:01,700
my career is finished.

1187
01:15:02,980 --> 01:15:06,210
Relax. We talked to some of the committee members.

1188
01:15:07,060 --> 01:15:09,490
A slap on the wrist, nothing more.

1189
01:15:10,060 --> 01:15:13,210
Is this the power of prayer, Tim, or money?

1190
01:15:13,300 --> 01:15:16,290
What power has the law where only money rules?

1191
01:15:18,100 --> 01:15:21,210
You know Deed will keep after us.
He won't stop.

1192
01:15:22,300 --> 01:15:23,450
Fine.

1193
01:15:35,940 --> 01:15:39,170
She's still there.
- What do I do?

1194
01:15:41,740 --> 01:15:43,770
Let's see how good she is.

1195
01:16:17,860 --> 01:16:19,650
She's not that good.

1196
01:16:27,100 --> 01:16:28,580
Has the committee finished?

1197
01:16:28,660 --> 01:16:31,220
No, Judge, it wasn't sitting today.

1198
01:16:52,220 --> 01:16:55,090
Are you all right? Are you okay?

1199
01:16:58,180 --> 01:17:01,930
- She's not responding.
- I'll call an ambulance.

1200
01:17:04,140 --> 01:17:07,850
An ambulance. Yeah, there's
been a road traffic accident.

1201
01:17:13,700 --> 01:17:15,650
It should have been me.

1202
01:17:16,740 --> 01:17:20,370
I'm sorry, Judge. I shouldn't
have let her drive the car.

1203
01:17:22,300 --> 01:17:24,490
We can't change things, Coop.

1204
01:17:29,420 --> 01:17:33,050
I've phoned both her sons and left messages.

1205
01:18:21,660 --> 01:18:23,890
Will the foreman please rise?

1206
01:18:26,500 --> 01:18:29,250
Have you reached a verdict
upon which you are all agreed?

1207
01:18:29,340 --> 01:18:30,610
We have.

1208
01:18:30,980 --> 01:18:34,890
On the count of manslaughter, do
you find the defendant guilty

1209
01:18:34,980 --> 01:18:36,460
or not guilty?

1210
01:18:36,540 --> 01:18:37,810
Guilty.

1211
01:18:37,900 --> 01:18:39,090
That's outrageous.

1212
01:18:44,140 --> 01:18:46,370
My lord, can we add a rider?

1213
01:18:47,180 --> 01:18:49,330
It'll have no force in law.

1214
01:18:49,420 --> 01:18:52,090
We feel there was a case of corporate manslaughter

1215
01:18:52,180 --> 01:18:55,890
against Hamling Productions and
the broadcaster to answer.

1216
01:18:57,500 --> 01:19:02,090
I will refer your recommendations to the
appropriate authorities. Thank you.

1217
01:19:08,140 --> 01:19:09,460
Celebrity.

1218
01:19:11,100 --> 01:19:16,490
The pursuit of the talentless by the mindless.

1219
01:19:17,780 --> 01:19:20,370
It's become a disease of the 21 St century.

1220
01:19:20,460 --> 01:19:24,610
It pollutes our society, it
diminishes all who seek it,

1221
01:19:24,700 --> 01:19:27,370
and all who worship it.

1222
01:19:28,020 --> 01:19:31,490
And you must bear part of the responsibility

1223
01:19:31,580 --> 01:19:35,620
for foisting this empty nonsense
onto a gullible public.

1224
01:19:39,100 --> 01:19:41,930
I'm going to adjourn sentencing
for reports, Mr Harrison?

1225
01:19:42,020 --> 01:19:44,330
- My lord, the question of bail...
- No.

1226
01:19:44,420 --> 01:19:47,370
No, I'm afraid I'm withdrawing
bail, Mr Mason Allen.

1227
01:19:47,460 --> 01:19:50,330
And you will explain to your client,

1228
01:19:50,420 --> 01:19:53,490
that a custodial sentence is almost inevitable.

1229
01:19:53,980 --> 01:19:55,610
All rise.

1230
01:20:27,940 --> 01:20:29,650
Dad.

1231
01:20:31,660 --> 01:20:33,410
Is there any change?

1232
01:20:44,340 --> 01:20:47,770
The Public Accounts Committee
found that Neil Haughton

1233
01:20:48,900 --> 01:20:51,970
was misguided in his friendship with Listfield.

1234
01:20:54,220 --> 01:20:57,170
But neither of them were found to be corrupt.

1235
01:21:10,460 --> 01:21:12,810
I think you were right about...

1236
01:21:17,900 --> 01:21:20,170
Mum's not going to give him up.

1237
01:21:28,860 --> 01:21:30,970
I don't have any evidence,

1238
01:21:33,580 --> 01:21:35,570
it's just a feeling.

1239
01:21:37,820 --> 01:21:42,290
With your leave, my lord, might I put the case
for my client, Mr Zand,

1240
01:21:42,380 --> 01:21:44,530
as to why this detention is unlawful.

1241
01:21:44,620 --> 01:21:47,730
Not unless you've got a new argument in law, no.

1242
01:21:48,940 --> 01:21:52,770
Are you ready to give up the
person who took the photograph?

1243
01:21:55,740 --> 01:21:58,730
Okay, dock officers, return them to the cells.

1244
01:22:02,380 --> 01:22:03,890
It was me, sir.

1245
01:22:05,780 --> 01:22:07,050
What's your name?

1246
01:22:07,140 --> 01:22:08,460
Jack Jones.

1247
01:22:08,540 --> 01:22:10,090
Right, everybody back.

1248
01:22:19,260 --> 01:22:22,250
Well, Mr Jones, it took us a
lot of time and inconvenience

1249
01:22:22,340 --> 01:22:24,290
to get here, didn't it?

1250
01:22:24,820 --> 01:22:26,610
I suggest you talk to your legal advisors

1251
01:22:26,700 --> 01:22:30,370
and I'll deal with you at the end of the day.
Dock officer.

1252
01:22:38,060 --> 01:22:41,730
Well, I suppose you feel that you've really flexed

1253
01:22:41,820 --> 01:22:45,010
your crusading muscles by your silence, don't you?

1254
01:22:46,660 --> 01:22:50,170
Unfortunately, you report news less and less,

1255
01:22:51,340 --> 01:22:53,570
and rarely fight for a better society.

1256
01:22:53,660 --> 01:22:57,650
Instead, most of you pander to
everything that's bad in our society.

1257
01:22:57,740 --> 01:23:00,610
In all this clamour around the TV Dungeon,

1258
01:23:00,700 --> 01:23:03,420
the worse, the more oafishly people behaved,

1259
01:23:03,500 --> 01:23:04,770
the more you pandered to them,

1260
01:23:04,860 --> 01:23:07,370
providing them with the celebrity that they crave.

1261
01:23:07,460 --> 01:23:11,770
Why don't you try resetting your targets
to something worthwhile?

1262
01:23:11,860 --> 01:23:14,890
Something that affects our
lives, like this government,

1263
01:23:14,980 --> 01:23:18,490
its ministers' sleazy
relationships with big business.

1264
01:23:18,580 --> 01:23:21,970
Try some hard targets with an independent voice,

1265
01:23:22,060 --> 01:23:25,370
instead of vacuously
door-stepping empty celebrity.

1266
01:23:27,100 --> 01:23:29,130
Go on, get out of here.

1267
01:23:41,940 --> 01:23:44,210
Do you need to be here, Judge?

1268
01:23:48,460 --> 01:23:51,490
John, any news on Mrs Mills?

1269
01:23:52,500 --> 01:23:54,410
Still unconscious.

1270
01:23:54,500 --> 01:23:58,490
Well, if you want me to help out with your work,
I'd be glad to.

1271
01:25:37,060 --> 01:25:40,330
We interviewed Sir Tim Listfield
at length, with his lawyer.

1272
01:25:40,420 --> 01:25:44,410
Needless to say, he denied all knowledge
of the man who attempted to kill you.

1273
01:25:44,500 --> 01:25:46,050
Steve Gross.

1274
01:25:46,780 --> 01:25:50,560
His mobile-phone records suggest
a very different story.

1275
01:25:50,660 --> 01:25:55,490
We'll keep working on him. He going
nowhere, Sir John, rest assured.

1276
01:25:58,860 --> 01:26:00,530
And Neil Haughton?

1277
01:26:03,340 --> 01:26:05,370
Not this time, Sir John.

1278
01:26:11,460 --> 01:26:12,650
Okay.

1279
01:26:32,380 --> 01:26:33,930
I love you, Jo.

1280
01:26:38,180 --> 01:26:39,660
So very much.

1281
01:27:38,500 --> 01:27:39,720
What?

